WEBVTT

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:10.480
<v Ira Glass>You know how it's been. In the Midwest, temperatures have been lower than at the South Pole. In Alabama, kids slept in schools. Highways shut down in Florida and Louisiana 'cause of ice.
<v Ira Glass>你知道情况一直是怎么样的。在中西部地区，气温比南极还低。在阿拉巴马州，孩子们睡在学校里。在佛罗里达州和路易斯安那州，由于结冰，高速公路被迫关闭。

00:00:10.480 --> 00:00:18.700
<v Ira Glass>Drivers in Atlanta ditched their cars and slept inside grocery stores. And in Southern California, it was just lovely.
<v Ira Glass>亚特兰大的司机们丢弃了他们的车，睡在杂货店里。而在南加州，天气却非常宜人。

00:00:18.700 --> 00:00:19.110
<v Ira Glass>[OCEAN WAVES]
<v Ira Glass>[海浪声]

00:00:19.110 --> 00:00:23.080
<v Sharon Jacobucci>Look at this! This is January! It's like 80! It's beautiful!
<v Sharon Jacobucci>看看这个！现在可是1月！气温有80华氏度（约27摄氏度）！太美了！

00:00:23.080 --> 00:00:31.800
<v Ira Glass>Sharon Jacobucci moved here from New York City years ago specifically to escape the cold. And she does not understand why the rest of us have not moved.
<v Ira Glass>莎伦·雅各布奇几年前从纽约市搬到这里，专门为了躲避寒冷。她不明白为什么我们其他人还没搬过来。

00:00:31.800 --> 00:00:44.450
<v Ira Glass>She has a job which puts her next to the beach all day long. A job that frankly, does not sound like a fun job. She gives out parking tickets. She's a meter maid. But 'cause it's on the beach, it makes her very, very happy.
<v Ira Glass>她有一份工作，让她整天待在海滩边。坦白说，这份工作听起来并不好玩。她负责开罚单。她是个贴罚单的。但因为是在海滩上，这让她感到非常、非常快乐。

00:00:44.450 --> 00:00:49.900
<v Sharon Jacobucci>I work all of Venice Beach. Villa Marina all the way to Navy Street.
<v Sharon Jacobucci>我负责整个威尼斯海滩。从玛丽娜别墅一直到海军街。

00:00:49.900 --> 00:00:55.590
<v Sharon Jacobucci>And I write tickets. I impound vehicles. I hug people. I say hello. I socialize.
<v Sharon Jacobucci>我写罚单。我扣押车辆。我拥抱人们。我打招呼。我进行社交。

00:00:55.590 --> 00:01:00.560
<v Sharon Jacobucci>I write more tickets. I impound vehicles. I answer radio call, and hug people some more.
<v Sharon Jacobucci>我写更多的罚单。我扣押车辆。我回复电台呼叫，然后再多拥抱一些人。

00:01:00.560 --> 00:01:04.120
<v Sharon Jacobucci>I work at the beach and get paid to go to the beach! You can't beat it!
<v Sharon Jacobucci>我在海滩工作，还能领着工资去海滩！没法比这更好的了！

00:01:04.120 --> 00:01:09.330
<v Ira Glass>Sharon was saying all this to one of our producers, Ben Calhoun. They were looking out onto the sand.
<v Ira Glass>莎伦在对我们的一位制片人本·卡尔霍恩说这一切。他们正眺望着沙滩。

00:01:09.330 --> 00:01:13.790
<v Sharon Jacobucci>I like it. And the water. Look at that water! Look at the sand!
<v Sharon Jacobucci>我喜欢它。还有水。看看那海水！看看那沙子！

00:01:13.790 --> 00:01:19.920
<v Sharon Jacobucci>It's very therapeutic, especially when you're writing tickets. Sometimes you just go stand out there and you look and you go, God is good.
<v Sharon Jacobucci>这非常有疗愈感，尤其是在你写罚单的时候。有时你只是站在那儿，看着它，然后你会想，上帝真是仁慈。

00:01:19.920 --> 00:01:24.450
<v Ben Calhoun>What do you feel when you look out that way? Out to the beach?
<v Ben Calhoun>当你朝那边看的时候，你有什么感觉？看向海滩那边？

00:01:24.450 --> 00:01:30.780
<v Sharon Jacobucci>What cute guy is out there? [LAUGHS] What cute guy is out there!
<v Sharon Jacobucci>那边有什么帅哥吗？[笑声] 那边有什么帅哥！

00:01:30.780 --> 00:01:35.900
<v Sharon Jacobucci>What do you want me to say? I'm looking at the men! We're at the beach! What else would I be looking at at the beach? [LAUGHS]
<v Sharon Jacobucci>你想让我说什么？我正在看男人！我们在海滩上！在海滩上我还能看什么？[笑声]

00:01:35.900 --> 00:01:42.730
<v Ira Glass>And right then, right on cue, maybe 10 feet away, a young surfer starts taking off his wetsuit.
<v Ira Glass>就在那时，说来就来，大概10英尺外，一位年轻的冲浪者开始脱下他的潜水服。

00:01:42.730 --> 00:01:51.630
<v Sharon Jacobucci>Just look at that! I get to watch the men strip! Look at him over there! Come on, honey! This is a great gig! [LAUGHS]
<v Sharon Jacobucci>看看那个！我能看着男人脱衣服！看他那边！来吧，亲爱的！这真是份好差事！[笑声]

00:01:51.630 --> 00:02:03.770
<v Ira Glass>Sharon lives five blocks from the beach. She told Ben that she knows everybody on Venice Beach. And then he walked with her on her rounds and it was like total love fest, nonstop greetings, blowing kisses.
<v Ira Glass>莎伦住在离海滩五个街区远的地方。她告诉本，她认识威尼斯海滩上的每一个人。然后他和她一起巡逻，感觉就像一场永不停歇的爱之庆典，不断的问候，飞吻。

00:02:03.770 --> 00:02:04.450
<v Sharon Jacobucci>[KISSING SOUNDS]
<v Sharon Jacobucci>[接吻声]

00:02:04.450 --> 00:02:06.030
<v Man>Don't listen to a word she says!
<v Man>别听她说的半个字！

00:02:06.030 --> 00:02:07.930
<v Sharon Jacobucci>I love you, baby. My honey bunny.
<v Sharon Jacobucci>我爱你，宝贝。我的小甜心。

00:02:07.930 --> 00:02:08.470
<v Woman>Morning, baby!
<v Woman>早上好，宝贝！

00:02:08.470 --> 00:02:11.210
<v Sharon Jacobucci>Good morning, baby! How are you?
<v Sharon Jacobucci>早上好，宝贝！你好吗？

00:02:11.210 --> 00:02:12.620
<v Woman>Fabulous. How  are you?
<v Woman>棒极了。你呢？

00:02:12.620 --> 00:02:13.800
<v Sharon Jacobucci>All right, darlin'.
<v Sharon Jacobucci>还可以，亲爱的。

00:02:13.800 --> 00:02:14.930
<v Ben Calhoun>You do know everybody.
<v Ben Calhoun>你确实认识每一个人。

00:02:14.930 --> 00:02:16.760
<v Sharon Jacobucci>I just told you that.
<v Sharon Jacobucci>我刚才就告诉你了。

00:02:16.760 --> 00:02:22.410
<v Ben Calhoun>Have you been watching the weather the last month or so with what's been going on in the rest of the country?
<v Ben Calhoun>你有没有关注过去一个月左右全国其他地区的天气情况？

00:02:22.410 --> 00:02:27.980
<v Sharon Jacobucci>I think nothing of it, baby. 'Cause I'm enjoying my sunshiny view in Venice.
<v Sharon Jacobucci>我对此一点也不关心，宝贝。因为我正在享受我在威尼斯的阳光美景。

00:02:27.980 --> 00:02:31.300
<v Sharon Jacobucci>I don't think anything about it. Could care less. That's why I moved here.
<v Sharon Jacobucci>我对此一点想法都没有。根本不在乎。这就是我搬到这里的原因。

00:02:31.300 --> 00:02:36.490
<v Sharon Jacobucci>If they don't like that, move your butts to Venice. Come to California. Hell, even go to Florida!
<v Sharon Jacobucci>如果他们不喜欢那样，就把你们的屁股挪到威尼斯来。来加州。见鬼，甚至去佛罗里达也行！

00:02:36.490 --> 00:02:38.090
<v Ben Calhoun>That's your message to them?
<v Ben Calhoun>这就是你要传达给他们的信息？

00:02:38.090 --> 00:02:44.730
<v Sharon Jacobucci>Yes! Pack your bags and move! Come to California! Come somewhere sunshiny beautiful!
<v Sharon Jacobucci>是的！打包行李搬过来！来加州！来一些阳光明媚美丽的地方！

00:02:44.730 --> 00:03:16.520
<v Ira Glass>Well, there's a lot of sympathy for you. Today on our radio show, we thought it would just be nice as we shiver, as we take our dogs for long walks in freezing temperatures, as we feel cold air leak in the windows and seep under our skins, we just thought it would be nice to get away from all that. For all of us who cannot take Sharon's advice and move to Venice Beach right this second, we can at least spend one hour thinking about places where it's warm. We can use the power of the radio to have a midwinter beach visit.
<v Ira Glass>好吧，大家都很同情你。今天在我们的广播节目中，我们想，当我们瑟瑟发抖，当我们带着狗在零下的温度中长距离散步，当我们感觉到冷空气从窗户缝渗入并刺入我们的皮肤时，想这些确实会很好。对于我们所有无法听从莎伦的建议立刻搬到威尼斯海滩的人，我们至少可以花一小时思考温暖的地方。我们可以利用广播的力量来进行一次隆冬的海滩之旅。

00:03:16.520 --> 00:03:21.970
<v Ira Glass>And so today on our radio show, let's just get away. Let's all get away. Let's go to the beach.
<v Ira Glass>所以今天在我们的广播节目中，让我们放松一下。让我们所有人都放松一下。让我们去海滩吧。

00:03:21.970 --> 00:03:30.930
<v Ira Glass>Friends, at Huntington and Malibu, they're shooting the pier. At Rincon, they're walking the nose. They're going on safari on the islands this year. So if you're coming, get ready to go.
<v Ira Glass>朋友们，在亨廷顿和马里布，他们正在冲浪。在林孔，他们正在练习脚尖冲浪。他们今年要去岛上探险。所以如果你要来，准备好出发吧。

00:03:30.930 --> 00:03:40.690
<v Ira Glass>From WBEZ Chicago, it's This American Life, distributed by Public Radio International. David Sedaris later this hour. Stay with us.
<v Ira Glass>这里是芝加哥WBEZ电台的《美国生活》，由国际公共广播电台发行。大卫·塞达里斯稍后会在本小时内出现。请继续收听。

00:03:40.690 --> 00:04:26.500
<v Ira Glass>[MUSIC - "KING OF THE BEACH" BY WAVVES]
<v Ira Glass>[音乐 - WAVVES乐队的《海滩之王》]

00:04:26.500 --> 00:04:29.230
<v Ira Glass>Act One, "Mexican Beach Doctor."
<v Ira Glass>第一幕，“墨西哥海滩医生”。

00:04:29.230 --> 00:04:30.910
<v Alex Blumberg>Oh hello? Yep, I'm here now.
<v Alex Blumberg>哦，喂？是的，我现在在这儿。

00:04:30.910 --> 00:04:31.880
<v Shane Dubow>Hey, Alex.
<v Shane Dubow>嘿，亚历克斯。

00:04:31.880 --> 00:04:33.960
<v Alex Blumberg>Hello, little buddy. How are you?
<v Alex Blumberg>你好，小伙计。你好吗？

00:04:33.960 --> 00:04:35.350
<v Shane Dubow>[LAUGHS] Hi, other little buddy.
<v Shane Dubow>[笑声] 嗨，另一个小伙计。

00:04:35.350 --> 00:04:35.710
<v Alex Blumberg>[LAUGHS]
<v Alex Blumberg>[笑声]

00:04:35.710 --> 00:04:43.080
<v Ira Glass>This first story takes place on a very warm beach in Mexico. Shane DuBow told it to our producer Alex Blumberg. They're old friends.
<v Ira Glass>第一个故事发生在一个墨西哥非常温暖的海滩上。谢恩·杜鲍告诉了我们的制片人亚历克斯·布隆伯格。他们是老朋友。

00:04:43.080 --> 00:05:01.430
<v Ira Glass>And as we were putting together this week's program, Alex remembered this thing that happened to Shane decades ago when Shane was on a kayaking trip in Mexico down in Baja California. Shane and his friends took a month long vacation. And every day, they would head out into the Gulf of California and go sea kayaking. They were really kind of in the middle of nowhere.
<v Ira Glass>当我们整理本周节目时，亚历克斯想起了几十年前谢恩在墨西哥下加利福尼亚州进行皮划艇旅行时发生的这件事。谢恩和他的朋友们休了一个月的假。每天，他们都会前往加利福尼亚湾进行海上皮划艇。他们基本上处于荒无人烟的地方。

00:05:01.430 --> 00:05:13.120
<v Shane Dubow>There's little beach communities and some little tourist centers. But mostly what we were doing was finding deserted open beaches and camping. So there was nobody around for most of the time.
<v Shane Dubow>有一些小的海滩社区和一些小的旅游中心。但我们大部分时间是在寻找荒凉的开放海滩并露营。所以大部分时间周围都没有人。

00:05:13.120 --> 00:05:17.510
<v Alex Blumberg>Wow. So it was just you--and there was how many of them was it you said? Seven? Six, seven?
<v Alex Blumberg>哇。所以只有你们——你说有多少人？七个？六个，七个？

00:05:17.510 --> 00:05:26.900
<v Shane Dubow>There was six or seven. And some people might go fishing, some people might play cards, some people might snorkel. I think we slept outside a lot.
<v Shane Dubow>有六或七个。有些人可能会去钓鱼，有些人可能会打牌，有些人可能会浮潜。我想我们经常睡在户外。

00:05:26.900 --> 00:05:44.020
<v Shane Dubow>It felt like we were 12 years old pretending to be Robinson Crusoe, living off the land. So we carried all our own water, all our own food, camping supplies, tents, sleeping bags, cooking supplies.
<v Shane Dubow>感觉我们就像12岁的孩子假装自己是鲁滨逊·克鲁索，靠土地为生。所以我们携带了自己所有的水、所有的食物、露营用品、帐篷、睡袋、烹饪用品。

00:05:44.020 --> 00:05:45.270
<v Alex Blumberg>Sunscreen, I hope.
<v Alex Blumberg>我希望带了防晒霜。

00:05:45.270 --> 00:05:48.600
<v Shane Dubow>Sunscreen? No, we were young then. We just figured it'd be fine.
<v Shane Dubow>防晒霜？不，那时我们还年轻。我们只是觉得会没事的。

00:05:48.600 --> 00:05:53.780
<v Alex Blumberg>[LAUGHING] Are you serious? You didn't bring any sunscreen?
<v Alex Blumberg>[笑声] 你是认真的吗？你们没带任何防晒霜？

00:05:53.780 --> 00:06:00.280
<v Shane Dubow>I'm sure I didn't.
<v Shane Dubow>我肯定没带。

00:06:00.280 --> 00:06:10.050
<v Shane Dubow>So we're in Baja. We are on a layover, which means we are just camped somewhere. We're not trying to kayak right now. And we've been clamming all day.
<v Shane Dubow>所以我们在下加利福尼亚州。我们在中途停留，这意味着我们只是在某个地方露营。我们现在不打算划皮划艇。我们整天都在挖蛤蜊。

00:06:10.050 --> 00:06:22.450
<v Shane Dubow>And my neck locks up and I can't turn my head to one side. And this is bad for lots of reasons. But when you're kayaking, you have to be able to paddle. You have to be able to use both arms.
<v Shane Dubow>然后我的脖子锁住了，我无法把头转向一边。这在很多方面都很糟糕。但当你划皮划艇时，你必须能够划桨。你必须能够使用双臂。

00:06:22.450 --> 00:06:25.500
<v Alex Blumberg>And this neck locking up, this is something that's happened to you periodically throughout your life.
<v Alex Blumberg>脖子锁住，这是你一生中周期性发生的事情。

00:06:25.500 --> 00:06:28.600
<v Shane Dubow>Yeah. This happens six to eight times a year.
<v Shane Dubow>是的。这每年发生六到八次。

00:06:28.600 --> 00:06:28.690
<v Alex Blumberg>OK.
<v Alex Blumberg>好的。

00:06:28.690 --> 00:06:30.550
<v Shane Dubow>It usually lasts three or four days.
<v Shane Dubow>它通常持续三到四天。

00:06:30.550 --> 00:06:30.980
<v Alex Blumberg>Right.
<v Alex Blumberg>对。

00:06:30.980 --> 00:07:00.960
<v Shane Dubow>But so no one wants to be stuck on the beach while I'm working out my neck for three or four days. So we had run into a little ex-pat community on a beach pretty close to where we were camping. These folks were living in campers and had set up little cantinas, which were really just stakes with a tarp over them and they would serve beers. Or in our case, they showed us how to clam and then they made us a clam feast.
<v Shane Dubow>但没有人想在海滩上被困住，而我在那里修复我的脖子三到四天。所以我们偶然发现了一个离我们露营地很近的海滩上的小外籍社区。这些人住在露营车里，并搭建了小餐馆，实际上只是用防水布盖着的木桩，他们会供应啤酒。或者就我们的情况而言，他们向我们展示了如何挖蛤蜊，然后为我们做了一顿蛤蜊盛宴。

00:07:00.960 --> 00:07:15.290
<v Shane Dubow>So we went back to the guy that had shown us how to do that, to his camper on the beach. And I said, there's not any chiropractors anywhere nearby? Which was just a ridiculous question, 'cause there was nothing nearby.
<v Shane Dubow>所以我们回到那个教我们的人那里，回到他在海滩上的露营车。我说，附近没有脊椎按摩师吗？这是一个荒谬的问题，因为附近什么都没有。

00:07:15.290 --> 00:07:15.560
<v Alex Blumberg>Right.
<v Alex Blumberg>对。

00:07:15.560 --> 00:07:36.010
<v Shane Dubow>And I mean, this is where it kind of gets apocryphal, but is actually true. 'Cause he gets this wistful look in his eye, and he says, no, there's no chiropractor. But there is an amateur chiropractor who helps some of the local people. And his name is Johnny Tequila.
<v Shane Dubow>我的意思是，这在某种程度上变成了杜撰，但实际上是真的。因为他的眼睛里露出了向往的神情，他说，没有，没有脊椎按摩师。但有一个业余脊椎按摩师，他帮助过一些当地人。他的名字叫强尼·龙舌兰。

00:07:36.010 --> 00:07:36.790
<v Alex Blumberg>[LAUGHING]
<v Alex Blumberg>[笑声]

00:07:36.790 --> 00:07:45.870
<v Shane Dubow>And he lives on a boat two coves over from where you're staying. And if you go to this man, he may help you.
<v Shane Dubow>他住在离你们住的地方隔两个海湾的一条船上。如果你去找这个人，他可能会帮助你。

00:07:45.870 --> 00:07:46.520
<v Alex Blumberg>[LAUGHING]
<v Alex Blumberg>[笑声]

00:07:46.520 --> 00:07:47.750
<v Shane Dubow>It was very mystical.
<v Shane Dubow>这非常神秘。

00:07:47.750 --> 00:07:51.130
<v Alex Blumberg>Right. And you were like, well, you had me at amateur chiropractor.
<v Alex Blumberg>对。而你当时的反应是，好吧，你用“业余脊椎按摩师”就把我吸引住了。

00:07:51.130 --> 00:07:51.490
<v Shane Dubow>[LAUGHING]
<v Shane Dubow>[笑声]

00:07:51.490 --> 00:07:54.290
<v Alex Blumberg>But once I find out that his name is Dr. Johnny Tequila--
<v Alex Blumberg>但当我发现他的名字叫强尼·龙舌兰博士时——

00:07:54.290 --> 00:08:01.060
<v Shane Dubow>[LAUGHING]
<v Shane Dubow>[笑声]

00:08:01.060 --> 00:08:02.860
<v Alex Blumberg>So you said, how do you get there?
<v Alex Blumberg>所以你问，怎么到那儿？

00:08:02.860 --> 00:08:08.230
<v Shane Dubow>How do I get there? I was like, I don't want to miss it. And he's like, you won't miss it. I was like, well, tell me where it is.
<v Shane Dubow>怎么到那儿？我就想，我不想错过它。他说，你不会错过的。我说，告诉我它在哪儿。

00:08:08.230 --> 00:08:29.580
<v Shane Dubow>And so he grabs a bar napkin and in black ballpoint pen I think, sketches me a rough outline of the coast, and puts an X. There's an X marks the spot. That's where Johnny Tequila is, two coves over. No one wants to go with me. They're all gonna chill out, play cards.
<v Shane Dubow>于是他抓起一张酒吧餐巾纸，我想用黑色圆珠笔，给我画了一个粗略的海岸轮廓，并画了一个X。X标记了位置。那就是强尼·龙舌兰所在的地方，隔两个海湾。没人想跟我一起去。他们都想放松一下，打打牌。

00:08:29.580 --> 00:08:32.200
<v Alex Blumberg>Oh, so you had to kayak up to this guy.
<v Alex Blumberg>哦，所以你不得不划皮划艇去找这个家伙。

00:08:32.200 --> 00:08:34.050
<v Shane Dubow>Yes. So I have to kayak to him.
<v Shane Dubow>是的。所以我得划皮划艇去找他。

00:08:34.050 --> 00:08:35.170
<v Alex Blumberg>I see.
<v Alex Blumberg>我明白了。

00:08:35.170 --> 00:08:43.770
<v Shane Dubow>And my friends had taken to teasing me about my paddle stroke, which was at this point one-armed and half crippled. And they were calling it the chicken wing.
<v Shane Dubow>我的朋友们开始嘲笑我的划桨动作，当时是一只手划，半残废状态。他们称之为“鸡翅膀”。

00:08:43.770 --> 00:08:44.930
<v Alex Blumberg>'Cause of your neck?
<v Alex Blumberg>因为你的脖子？

00:08:44.930 --> 00:08:46.020
<v Shane Dubow>'Cause of my neck.
<v Shane Dubow>因为我的脖子。

00:08:46.020 --> 00:08:46.330
<v Alex Blumberg>Yeah.
<v Alex Blumberg>对。

00:08:46.330 --> 00:08:59.070
<v Shane Dubow>So I chicken wing for two coves worth. Maybe a mile paddle. And you're really close to the shore, and the beach is right on your right and the open ocean is on your left. And you're chicken winging.
<v Shane Dubow>所以我划了两个海湾的“鸡翅膀”。可能有一英里的路程。你离岸边非常近，右边是海滩，左边是广阔的海洋。而你在划“鸡翅膀”。

00:08:59.070 --> 00:09:06.820
<v Shane Dubow>One cove, OK. And I'm looking at my-- and my napkin gets wet, so my map gets all destroyed, comes apart.
<v Shane Dubow>一个海湾，好的。我看着我的——我的餐巾纸湿了，所以地图全毁了，散开了。

00:09:06.820 --> 00:09:09.920
<v Alex Blumberg>[LAUGHING] 'Cause the water's running down your paddle handle?
<v Alex Blumberg>[笑声] 因为水顺着你的桨柄流下来了？

00:09:09.920 --> 00:09:20.600
<v Shane Dubow>So I chicken wing on over. And there in the second cove, is a catamaran in the middle of an empty cove.
<v Shane Dubow>所以我划着“鸡翅膀”过去了。在第二个海湾里，有一个双体船停在空旷的海湾中间。

00:09:20.600 --> 00:09:38.350
<v Shane Dubow>And I don't want you to get the idea that this is a harbor or a dock or anything man made. Nothing around. And it's docked in the water maybe 20 or 30 yards from shore. And as I paddle closer, I don't see anyone.
<v Shane Dubow>我不希望你认为这是一个港口、码头或任何人工建筑。周围什么都没有。它停在离岸边大概20或30码的水里。当我划近时，我没看到任何人。

00:09:38.350 --> 00:09:38.550
<v Alex Blumberg>Mm hmm.
<v Alex Blumberg>嗯嗯。

00:09:38.550 --> 00:09:52.980
<v Shane Dubow>It's got a cabin. But the mast is up, no sail is up. And as I get closer and closer, I can see around the mast, lined up, are empty Cuervo Gold tequila bottles.
<v Shane Dubow>它有一个船舱。但桅杆是竖着的，没有帆。当我越来越近时，我可以看到桅杆周围，排成一排的是空的夸尔沃金牌龙舌兰酒瓶。

00:09:52.980 --> 00:09:53.660
<v Alex Blumberg>[LAUGHING]
<v Alex Blumberg>[笑声]

00:09:53.660 --> 00:10:03.690
<v Shane Dubow>But kind of orderly. That was the weird thing. Usually you don't associate empty tequila bottles with order. But these had been meticulously lined up.
<v Shane Dubow>但排列得挺整齐。这很奇怪。通常你不会把空龙舌兰酒瓶和秩序联系起来。但这些都被精心排列好了。

00:10:03.690 --> 00:10:04.920
<v Alex Blumberg>Ringing the mast.
<v Alex Blumberg>围绕着桅杆。

00:10:04.920 --> 00:10:21.270
<v Shane Dubow>Ringing the mast. And again, I'm paddling up on a boat in the middle of nowhere with no one else around. And I don't really know how to even start. And from some deep place, the word that comes to me is ahoy.
<v Shane Dubow>围绕着桅杆。再说一次，我划船靠近一个身处荒郊野外的船，周围没有其他人。我甚至不知道该怎么开始。从内心深处，我蹦出的一个词是“船上的人！”。

00:10:21.270 --> 00:10:22.160
<v Alex Blumberg>[LAUGHING]
<v Alex Blumberg>[笑声]

00:10:22.160 --> 00:10:30.370
<v Shane Dubow>[LAUGHING] Which I've certainly never used in normal conversation. So I say, ahoy!
<v Shane Dubow>[笑声] 我在正常交谈中肯定从没用过这个词。所以我喊道，船上的人！

00:10:30.370 --> 00:10:50.540
<v Shane Dubow>And from out of the cabin comes a completely naked woman. She looks American. Blond hair, tanned so deeply, it's like the tan that goes to your liver. It's just tan all the way through.
<v Shane Dubow>然后从船舱里走出来一个完全赤裸的女人。她看起来像个美国人。金色的头发，晒得非常深，那种晒到了肝脏的颜色。就是完全晒透了。

00:10:50.540 --> 00:11:14.150
<v Shane Dubow>Really muscly. Her shoulders looked like she was probably a rafting guide in Colorado. So she's completely naked and completely unphased about being completely naked. Just greets me and talks to me as if she wearing clothes. And she's above me, so I'm just looking up at her being naked from my kayak holding on to the side of their boat.
<v Shane Dubow>非常健壮。她的肩膀看起来像是科罗拉多州的漂流向导。所以她完全赤裸，并且对赤裸这件事完全无动于衷。就像她穿着衣服一样跟我打招呼和交谈。她在我上方，所以我就从我的皮划艇里仰视着她赤裸的样子，扶着她们船的侧面。

00:11:14.150 --> 00:11:34.290
<v Shane Dubow>And I'm in my kayak and I said, is Johnny Tequila here? And she's very nice, and she goes, no, he went to town for supplies. But he'll be back shortly. Why don't you wait until he comes back?
<v Shane Dubow>我在我的皮划艇里，我说，强尼·龙舌兰在这儿吗？她非常友善，她说，不，他进城去拿物资了。但他很快就会回来。你为什么不等他回来呢？

00:11:34.290 --> 00:11:43.580
<v Shane Dubow>And then eventually Johnny Tequila, we see him on the beach near us. And he's got a little rowboat and he rows back to us.
<v Shane Dubow>然后最后强尼·龙舌兰，我们在附近的沙滩上看到了他。他有一条小划艇，然后划船回到我们这里。

00:11:43.580 --> 00:11:56.800
<v Shane Dubow>And he looks exactly like her. I mean, he's got on shorts, but he's got that tan. He looks kind of muscly in his shoulders and chest, and they both have wild, bleached out blond hair and real scruffy.
<v Shane Dubow>他看起来和她一模一样。我的意思是，他穿着短裤，但他有那种晒痕。他的肩膀和胸部看起来很健壮，他们俩都有野性、漂白的金发，而且看起来很邋遢。

00:11:56.800 --> 00:12:10.350
<v Shane Dubow>Maybe 30s? Although the sun makes everyone look older, so who knows? And I tell him my story and he's like, yeah of course I'll perform some amateur chiropractory on your neck.
<v Shane Dubow>大概30多岁？虽然太阳让每个人看起来都更老，谁知道呢？我告诉他我的故事，他说，是的，当然我会对你的脖子进行一些业余脊椎按摩。

00:12:10.350 --> 00:12:14.860
<v Shane Dubow>He didn't say that. He said just yeah, of course I'll help you. Follow me to shore.
<v Shane Dubow>他没那么说。他说只是是的，当然我会帮你。跟我到岸上去。

00:12:14.860 --> 00:12:24.800
<v Shane Dubow>So he rows and I chicken wing to the shore, and we pull our boats up. And then he said, follow me.
<v Shane Dubow>于是他划船，我划着“鸡翅膀”到岸边，我们把船拉上来。然后他说，跟着我。

00:12:24.800 --> 00:12:47.290
<v Shane Dubow>And now we are going-- I want to say jungle, but it was not jungle. But it's dense scrub. I mean, there's bushes all around us, there's cacti. Winding around in the middle of Mexico with no one else for miles, and I'm following him on this path, a really faint path.
<v Shane Dubow>现在我们要去——我想说丛林，但这根本不是丛林。但那是茂密的灌木丛。我的意思是，我们周围都是灌木，有仙人掌。在墨西哥中部没人住的地方绕来绕去，我跟着他走在这一条小路上，一条非常模糊的小路。

00:12:47.290 --> 00:12:59.590
<v Shane Dubow>And we come to a clearing. And in the clearing, there is a table exactly like a massage table or a chiropractic table you'd see in a real chiropractor's office.
<v Shane Dubow>我们来到一块空地。在那块空地上，有一张桌子，和你会在真正的脊椎按摩师诊所看到的按摩桌或脊椎按摩桌一模一样。

00:12:59.590 --> 00:13:20.350
<v Shane Dubow>With the center part that's open and you could put your face down there. And the neck part articulates and comes up. You know, it's the real deal. And a life size human skeleton hanging from a tree. Which I assume is a replica. But it looks like it.
<v Shane Dubow>中间部分是空的，你可以把脸放下去。颈部部分可以活动并向上抬起。你知道，那是真家伙。还有一个人体骨架挂在树上。我想那是复制品。但看起来很像。

00:13:20.350 --> 00:13:47.600
<v Shane Dubow>A skeleton and the table in a clearing in the middle of the desert in Mexico. And then has me lie on my back looking up at his face and his crazy hair. And he's shirtless. Did I tell you my chiropractor is shirtless? He's shirtless. And he puts his hands around my neck.
<v Shane Dubow>在墨西哥沙漠中央的空地上，一个骨架和一张桌子。然后他让我躺在背上，看着他的脸和他那疯狂的头发。他是赤膊的。我有没有告诉你我的脊椎按摩师是赤膊的？他是赤膊的。他把手放在我的脖子上。

00:13:47.600 --> 00:14:09.000
<v Shane Dubow>In the middle of Mexico, in a clearing with a skeleton, my amateur chiropractor now has my neck in his hands. And he gives me a chiropractic exam that resembles every other chiropractic exam I've received. And then he does an adjustment that also passes as any other chiropractic adjustment I've received.
<v Shane Dubow>在墨西哥中部，在一个挂着骨架的空地上，我的业余脊椎按摩师现在把我的脖子放在了他的手里。他给我做了一次脊椎按摩检查，就像我接受过的任何其他脊椎按摩检查一样。然后他做了一次调整，也和我在其他地方接受的任何脊椎按摩调整一样。

00:14:09.000 --> 00:14:19.770
<v Shane Dubow>And I say, Johnny Tequila, thank you for adjusting my neck. Can I pay you?
<v Shane Dubow>我说，强尼·龙舌兰，谢谢你调整我的脖子。我可以付钱给你吗？

00:14:19.770 --> 00:14:30.550
<v Shane Dubow>And he said, no. I just do this to help people. There will be no payment. But if you ever see me in a bar, you can buy me a shot of tequila.
<v Shane Dubow>他说，不。我这样做只是为了帮助别人。不会有任何报酬。但如果你在酒吧见到我，你可以请我喝一杯龙舌兰酒。

00:14:30.550 --> 00:14:38.590
<v Alex Blumberg>[LAUGHING] Then the next day, was in fact your neck better?
<v Alex Blumberg>[笑声] 那么第二天，你的脖子真的好点了吗？

00:14:38.590 --> 00:14:56.450
<v Shane Dubow>Well, it's possible I'm collapsing time. But the way I remember it, I chicken wing back and over the next few hours, I start to feel much, much better. And the neck is OK.
<v Shane Dubow>好吧，我可能把时间搞混了。但据我记忆，我划着“鸡翅膀”回去，在接下来的几个小时里，我开始感觉好多了。脖子也没事了。

00:14:56.450 --> 00:15:11.310
<v Alex Blumberg>Do you think that Johnny Tequila-- when you think of Johnny Tequila, is he an argument for chucking it all and moving to some quiet beach in some distant land or is he an argument against it?
<v Alex Blumberg>你认为强尼·龙舌兰——当你想到强尼·龙舌兰时，他是为了抛弃一切搬到遥远土地上某个宁静的海滩提供了一个论据，还是反对它的论据？

00:15:11.310 --> 00:15:29.230
<v Shane Dubow>He's 100% an argument for. I can't believe you asked me that question. A simpler life. Just crack people's necks, drink tequila, sing in the cantina, and go home to my naked lady.
<v Shane Dubow>他是100%赞成的论据。我不敢相信你问我那个问题。更简单的生活。只要帮人整整脖子，喝喝龙舌兰酒，在餐馆里唱唱歌，回家找我的赤裸女郎。

00:15:29.230 --> 00:15:32.080
<v Shane Dubow>Did I not tell the story to make it seem good? It seemed great!
<v Shane Dubow>难道我没把这个故事讲得听起来很好吗？这看起来棒极了！

00:15:32.080 --> 00:15:39.920
<v Alex Blumberg>[LAUGHING]
<v Alex Blumberg>[笑声]

00:15:39.920 --> 00:15:44.160
<v Ira Glass>Shane DuBow talking to Alex Blumberg in Chicago.
<v Ira Glass>谢恩·杜鲍在芝加哥与亚历克斯·布隆伯格交谈。

00:15:44.160 --> 00:16:08.090
<v Ira Glass>[MUSIC - "JOHNNY" BY AB & THE SEA]
<v Ira Glass>[音乐 - AB & THE SEA的《强尼》]

00:16:08.090 --> 00:16:18.160
<v Ira Glass>Act Two, "Long Talk on a Short Pier." Sam Mullins' friend Stesha was born in same month as he was, in the same hospital. Their moms were best friends.
<v Ira Glass>第二幕，“短码头上的长谈”。萨姆·马林斯的朋友斯特莎和他出生在同一个月，在同一家医院。他们的妈妈是最好的朋友。

00:16:18.160 --> 00:16:31.620
<v Ira Glass>So of course, when they were little kids, they became best friends. You know those bathtub pictures that parents take of their kids? There are bathtub shots of Sam and Stesha back when they were little toddlers growing up in this town called Vernon in Canada.
<v Ira Glass>所以当然，当他们是小孩子的时候，他们就成了最好的朋友。你知道父母给孩子拍的那些浴盆照片吗？有萨姆和斯特莎小时候在加拿大弗农这个小镇长大的浴盆照片。

00:16:31.620 --> 00:16:40.120
<v Ira Glass>Anyway, one day his best friend Stesha moves away. He's devastated, talks to her now and then. A couple of years pass, until one summer.
<v Ira Glass>总之，有一天他最好的朋友斯特莎搬走了。他崩溃了，偶尔和她通通电话。几年过去了，直到一个夏天。

00:16:40.120 --> 00:16:48.680
<v Ira Glass>Sam told what happened that summer on stage at a storytelling event called "The Flame." It was the summer before he started high school, before he turned 13.
<v Ira Glass>萨姆在一次名为“火焰”的故事讲述活动舞台上讲述了那个夏天发生的事情。那是他上高中之前，在他13岁之前。

00:16:48.680 --> 00:17:17.160
<v Sam Mullins>Which is a really big summer. Because if you think about it, you need to prepare yourself to go from a building with finger paintings on the walls to go into a new building with anti-drug ads, and there's like condom dispensers in the bathrooms. And when you're sitting in the classroom looking out the window, instead of the familiar jungle gym with a slide, there's four guys wearing bandannas hot boxing in an Acura Integra.
<v Sam Mullins>这是一个非常重要的夏天。因为如果你仔细想想，你需要为自己做准备，从一栋墙上贴着手指画的建筑，去往一栋有反毒品广告的新建筑，而且厕所里有避孕套自动贩卖机。当你坐在教室里向窗外看时，看到的不是熟悉的有滑梯的攀爬架，而是四个戴着头巾的家伙在一辆讴歌Integra里吞云吐雾。

00:17:17.160 --> 00:17:43.210
<v Sam Mullins>So my plan for the summer was pretty clear. I needed to get cool fast. I wanted to wear the right things, to think the right things, to do the right things. I wasn't even concerned about having my own unique style. That first year, I just wanted to walk in already the epitome of what a high school boy was.
<v Sam Mullins>所以那个夏天的计划很明确。我需要快速变得酷起来。我想穿对的衣服，想对的事，做对的事。我甚至不关心是否有自己独特的风格。第一年，我只是想走进去时就已经是一个高中男生的缩影。

00:17:43.210 --> 00:17:51.710
<v Sam Mullins>So I went out and I bought a skateboard. And I was really bad at it, so I just carried it around with me everywhere. It was more of an accessory than anything.
<v Sam Mullins>所以我出去买了一个滑板。但我玩得很差，所以我只是把它随身携带。比起其他任何东西，它更像是一个配件。

00:17:51.710 --> 00:18:14.660
<v Sam Mullins>And I started listening to some new music. And I was doing push ups and sit ups and learning some guitar. And I was learning how to handle my voice cracking. The secrets to not having your changing voice crack is to just not be excited or enthusiastic about anything, ever.
<v Sam Mullins>然后我开始听一些新音乐。我在做俯卧撑和仰卧起坐，学弹吉他。我在学习如何处理我变声时的声音。让变声期声音不破音的秘诀就是永远不要对任何事情感到兴奋或热情。

00:18:14.660 --> 00:18:33.970
<v Sam Mullins>I needed to learn how to play it cool, so that I'd be ready to talk to girls. Pardon me. To talk to women. So it was in the middle of this get cool fast regimen when one day my mom burst in. And she's like, hey Stesha's on the phone! Pick it up!
<v Sam Mullins>我需要学习如何表现得酷，这样我就准备好和女孩交谈了。抱歉。和女人交谈。所以正是在这个“快速变酷”方案的过程中，有一天我妈妈冲了进来。她说，嘿，斯特莎在电话上！接电话！

00:18:33.970 --> 00:18:56.470
<v Sam Mullins>So I pick it up and Stesha's like, hey Sammy! Guess what? I'm coming to visit for a few weeks, and I'm actually going to be in town for my birthday! And I was wondering, since I don't have a house in Vernon anymore, and since you live on the lake, I was thinking maybe I could have my birthday party at your house.
<v Sam Mullins>我接起电话，斯特莎说，嘿萨米！你猜怎么着？我要去拜访几个星期，而且生日那天我正好会在镇上！我在想，既然我在弗农已经没有房子了，而且既然你住在湖边，我在想也许我可以在你家举办生日派对。

00:18:56.470 --> 00:19:20.240
<v Sam Mullins>And my mind was reeling. This was perfect. Because the best part of having a girl for a best friend is the birthday parties. At every one of Stesha's birthday parties growing up, I was always the only guy. And I used to act like it was a drag, but it was the best.
<v Sam Mullins>我的头脑在飞速运转。这太完美了。因为有一个女孩作为最好的朋友，最好的部分就是生日派对。在我成长的每一次斯特莎的生日派对上，我总是唯一的男人。我过去常表现得好像那是一种负担，但那实际上是最棒的。

00:19:20.240 --> 00:19:36.460
<v Sam Mullins>It was wonderful. So now Stesha's asking me if I wouldn't mind if she brings her 20 beautiful friends over to my-- her beautiful French immersion friends. Which in Vernon, that's as exotic as it gets.
<v Sam Mullins>真是太棒了。所以现在斯特莎问我，如果她带她那20个漂亮的朋友来我家，我介不介意？她那些漂亮的法语沉浸式班的朋友。在弗农，那已经是最异国情调的了。

00:19:36.460 --> 00:19:59.570
<v Sam Mullins>So they're gonna come over and put on their bathing suits and swim at my house? And I don't have to lift a finger? This was perfect to me. And this whole birthday would serve as a test at the end of the summer where I can prove to myself that I am cool, and I am ready to talk to girls.
<v Sam Mullins>所以她们要来我家换上泳衣游泳？我甚至连一根手指头都不用动？这对我是完美的。而整个生日派对将作为夏末的一次测试，我可以向自己证明我很酷，我已经准备好和女孩交谈了。

00:19:59.570 --> 00:20:21.590
<v Sam Mullins>So it's the day of the party and all the pretty girls are showing up. And everything's going great. I'm playing them a burnt CD I just burnt on my new CD burner. It was all the coolest music I could think of. It was mostly just Blink-182.
<v Sam Mullins>派对当天，所有漂亮的女孩都出现了。一切都很顺利。我给她们放了一张我刚用新CD刻录机烧录的CD。那是我能想到的最酷的音乐。大部分只是Blink-182乐队的歌。

00:20:21.590 --> 00:20:49.960
<v Sam Mullins>And we were all swimming in the lake and eating barbecue. And I was talking to the girls and catching up with Stesha. And I was doing my best to sit in a semi-reclined position with the sun hitting me at the right angle to accentuate my abdominal muscles.
<v Sam Mullins>我们都在湖里游泳和吃烧烤。我当时在和女孩们聊天，和斯特莎叙旧。我正在尽我所能以半躺的姿势坐着，让阳光以正确的角度照在我身上，以突显我的腹肌。

00:20:49.960 --> 00:21:04.500
<v Sam Mullins>So it's a little bit later on, and all the girls are out on the dock and they're all laying in the sun. And two of them are at the end of the dock and they're hitting a volleyball back and forth and they hit the ball into the water.
<v Sam Mullins>过了一会儿，女孩们都在码头上，她们都躺在阳光下。其中两个在码头尽头，她们在来回打排球，球掉进了水里。

00:21:04.500 --> 00:21:28.430
<v Sam Mullins>And they're all like, ugh, I don't want to go in the lake! I'm all warm and dry now! And I'm like, oh my God, this is my chance! Time to be a hero. So I stand up and I'm like, "I'll get that for you." And I dramatically take off my sunglasses.
<v Sam Mullins>她们都说，呃，我不想下湖！我全身都暖和又干爽了！我说，哦我的上帝，这就是我的机会！是时候成为英雄了。我站起来，说，“我帮你们去拿。”我戏剧性地摘下了太阳镜。

00:21:28.430 --> 00:21:58.170
<v Sam Mullins>And my plan was not to merely just hop in the water and retrieve the ball. No, no, no. My plan was to run, to sprint as fast as I can and jump as far as I can. Because even at that age, I knew that that's what women really want is someone that can run and jump far.
<v Sam Mullins>我的计划不仅仅是跳进水里取回球。不，不，不。我的计划是奔跑，以最快的速度冲刺，尽可能跳得最远。因为即使在那样的年纪，我也知道女人真正想要的是一个能奔跑且跳得很远的人。

00:21:58.170 --> 00:22:13.690
<v Sam Mullins>Right, girls? So I took off. And I'm running down the center of the dock and there's girls on both sides and everyone's watching me. And I might as well have had a cape on I felt like such a hero.
<v Sam Mullins>对吧，姑娘们？所以我跑了。我跑在码头的中心，女孩们在两边，每个人都在看着我。我觉得自己就像披着斗篷一样，感觉自己像个英雄。

00:22:13.690 --> 00:22:41.490
<v Sam Mullins>I'm running down the dock, and with a few feet of dock left, trouble. Because we were swimming for a couple of hours, so the surface of the dock was really wet and slippery. And I slipped and I hit the deck, literally. But I had enough momentum from my heroic sprint that I was now sliding on the surface of the dock.
<v Sam Mullins>我顺着码头跑，还剩几英尺时，麻烦来了。因为我们游泳了几个小时，所以码头表面非常湿滑。我滑倒了，真的撞到了甲板上。但我刚才的英雄式冲刺带来的惯性使我开始在码头表面滑动。

00:22:41.490 --> 00:22:53.700
<v Sam Mullins>Now had I just slid off the edge and into the water, it would've been totally fine. I would've been like, "Pshaw! That didn't even hurt. I'm gonna go and get the ball now."
<v Sam Mullins>如果我只是滑出边缘掉进水里，那完全没问题。我会说，“嘘！那一点也不疼。我现在去拿球。”

00:22:53.700 --> 00:23:13.690
<v Sam Mullins>But that isn't what happened. Because there was a nail up. And the nail cut me. From here to about here. And about here, it caught on to my bathing suit.
<v Sam Mullins>但事情并非如此。因为有一根钉子露出来。那根钉子割伤了我。从这里到这里。然后在大约这个位置，它钩住了我的泳裤。

00:23:13.690 --> 00:23:36.150
<v Sam Mullins>Now had the bathing suit ripped and then I fell in, it would've been OK. Or even if it fully caught my suit and it ejected me from the suit naked into the lake, I truly believe that I could've brought it back. That would've been OK.
<v Sam Mullins>如果泳裤撕裂然后我掉下去，那也没问题。甚至如果它完全钩住我的泳裤，把我赤身裸体地抛进湖里，我真心相信我能挽回局面。那也会没事的。

00:23:36.150 --> 00:23:47.770
<v Sam Mullins>I would've been like, "Wow, what a freak accident! Could you pass me my trunks? I'm gonna go and get the ball now."
<v Sam Mullins>我会说，“哇，真是个奇怪的事故！能把泳裤递给我吗？我现在去拿球。”

00:23:47.770 --> 00:24:28.950
<v Sam Mullins>But that isn't what happened. Because the good people at Quicksilver make too fine a product and my trunks didn't rip. So what happened was I went over the edge and I swung and I hit my head on the pillar. And my legs were straight up in the air kicking like mad. And I'm splashing around. My head is half underwater, and my penis is out.
<v Sam Mullins>但事情并非如此。因为Quicksilver的好人们制造了质量太好的产品，我的裤子没破。所以我翻过了边缘，我摆动了一下，头撞在了柱子上。我的双腿在空中疯狂地乱踢。我在水里乱拍。我的头有一半在水下，而我的阴茎露了出来。

00:24:28.950 --> 00:24:47.000
<v Sam Mullins>So I'm upside down and I think I'm drowning and I don't know what's happening. I'm in fight or flight, I'm splashing. And what does one do when you're in what you perceive to be a life and death situation?
<v Sam Mullins>所以我倒挂着，以为自己快淹死了，不知道发生了什么。我在战斗或逃跑状态中，我在乱拍。当你处于你认为的生死攸关的情况时，你会怎么做？

00:24:47.000 --> 00:25:00.610
<v Sam Mullins>"Mom! Mommy! Help Mom! Help Mom!"
<v Sam Mullins>“妈妈！妈咪！救救我妈！救救我妈！”

00:25:00.610 --> 00:25:26.990
<v Sam Mullins>And what happened next I have to give to you second hand, because I was underwater. But legend has it that my mom was inside making a potato salad. And she heard me calling for help and she snapped into action and she knocked the screen sliding door off the track and she was hurdling over lawn furniture and weaving through girls. And she got to me and she pulled me to safety in a matter of seconds.
<v Sam Mullins>接下来发生的事我只能从别人口中转述，因为我当时在水下。但传说我妈妈当时在里面做土豆沙拉。她听到我呼救，立刻采取行动，把纱门从轨道上撞了下来，她跨过草坪家具，穿过人群。她在几秒钟内赶到我身边并把我拉到了安全地带。

00:25:26.990 --> 00:25:43.780
<v Sam Mullins>And I was so confused. I was like, what, what the [BLEEP]? [BLEEP]. What the [BLEEP]? And I'll always remember, my mom whispered in my ear, "Honey, quit cursing."
<v Sam Mullins>我当时非常困惑。我当时在想，怎么，怎么回事？[哔]。什么鬼？我永远记得，我妈妈在我耳边低语，“亲爱的，别说脏话。”

00:25:43.780 --> 00:26:12.480
<v Sam Mullins>And I'm covered in blood and I'm shaking. And I remember I turned my attention to the girls halfheartedly trying to conceal their laughter. And I looked out at the lake and I could see the ball. And it was long gone, it was floating away. Thank you.
<v Sam Mullins>我浑身是血，在颤抖。我记得我漫不经心地看向那些试图掩饰笑声的女孩们。我看向湖面，我能看到球。它早就没影了，漂走了。谢谢。

00:26:12.480 --> 00:26:17.300
<v Ira Glass>Sam Mullins. He's a comedian and comedy writer in Vancouver.
<v Ira Glass>萨姆·马林斯。他是温哥华的一位喜剧演员和喜剧编剧。

00:26:17.300 --> 00:26:45.830
