<v Ira Glass>[音乐 - THE TWO MAN GENTLEMAN BAND的《泳池派对》]

00:26:45.830 --> 00:27:03.700
<v Ira Glass>Act Three, "The Beachcomber." OK, so the absolute beachiest radio show I think maybe that has ever been made-- I believe, anyway-- was done by my first boss at NPR, this documentary producer named Keith Talbot. He made this back in 1979. It was called Ocean Hour.
<v Ira Glass>第三幕，“海滩拾荒者”。好的，我认为这大概是史上制作过的最具有海滩风情的广播节目了——总之我相信是这样——这是我刚进NPR时的老板，一位名叫基思·塔尔博特的纪录片制作人做的。他在1979年制作了这个节目。它叫《海洋时刻》。

00:27:03.700 --> 00:27:12.310
<v Ira Glass>And I love that show. Like I say, I think it's beachiest radio show mankind will ever produce, and here is one of my favorite parts.
<v Ira Glass>我喜欢那个节目。就像我说的那样，我认为它是人类有史以来制作过的最具有海滩风情的广播节目，这是我最喜欢的部分之一。

00:27:12.310 --> 00:27:29.840
<v Ira Glass>Just before I play this, I should say this is such a piece of 1970s era public radio that there's no narration on the show giving the name of the interviewee who you're about to hear or where he is. That's how we rolled back then. Anyway, I hope you like this.
<v Ira Glass>在播放之前，我应该说这是70年代公共广播的一个典型片段，节目中没有解说给出你即将听到的受访者的名字或他在哪里。那就是我们当时的风格。总之，我希望你喜欢这个。

00:27:29.840 --> 00:27:36.720
<v Beachcomber>Here is a scrounger's life. Everything is like a beachcomber.
<v Beachcomber>这就是拾荒者的生活。一切都像个海滩拾荒者。

00:27:36.720 --> 00:27:45.220
<v Beachcomber>I have lived as poor as a man can live and still survive in United States of America in the 1970s. Because everything comes here somehow sooner or later.
<v Beachcomber>我曾生活得像个在70年代的美国依然能生存的最穷的人。因为一切不知何故迟早都会来到这里。

00:27:45.220 --> 00:28:02.010
<v Beachcomber>Wood, oysters, the various other fish that you can get for the asking. Mullet, which is the big, good eating fish around here, along with trout and catfish, which come from the rivers. You have the river cat and the salt water cat. They seem to be cousins, but they taste a little different.
<v Beachcomber>木头、牡蛎，以及你可以随手得到的各种其他鱼类。鲻鱼，这是这里最棒的食用鱼，还有鳟鱼和鲶鱼，它们来自河流。你有河鲶和海鲶。它们似乎是表亲，但味道有点不同。

00:28:02.010 --> 00:28:16.460
<v Beachcomber>And in addition to all of that, you get the kinds of things that the sea brings, which is from boats. There's always a certain amount of movement of cargo in boats as well as fish.
<v Beachcomber>除了所有这些，你还能得到大海带来的东西，来自船只。船上的货物和鱼类总是有一定的流动。

00:28:16.460 --> 00:28:29.860
<v Beachcomber>And firewood around here is for the asking. In a city like New York or Washington, firewood I'm sure costs $100 a truckload, $200. Round here you can go out and fill it up for nothing.
<v Beachcomber>这里的木柴随手可得。在纽约或华盛顿这样的城市，木柴肯定要100美元一卡车，200美元。在这里你可以免费装满它。

00:28:29.860 --> 00:28:46.680
<v Beachcomber>There is a theory, for example, in archaeology that all of the early settlements were on the coast of man. That that's where the easiest life has always been, where everything was for the taking and you had the sea and you had the land together and the various mutations between them.
<v Beachcomber>例如，考古学中有一种理论认为，所有早期定居点都在人类的海岸线上。那一直是最简单的生活方式，你可以随意获取一切，你拥有海洋和陆地，以及它们之间的各种变异。

00:28:46.680 --> 00:28:58.440
<v Beachcomber>And that's where food chains likely start, where you have that melting land or that solidifying water. That's where the organisms get their start, especially if you have the right climate.
<v Beachcomber>那很可能是食物链开始的地方，在那里你拥有融化的陆地或固化的水。那就是生物开始的地方，特别是如果你有合适的气候。

00:28:58.440 --> 00:29:12.260
<v Beachcomber>As a poor man, it means that things are coming to me which don't happen very much on the prairie or in the big cities. Nothing comes to your door in a big city except a cop or a taxi driver or something. But here things will actually come to your door.
<v Beachcomber>作为一个穷人，这意味着东西会来到我面前，而在大草原或大城市里这种情况不会经常发生。在大城市里，除了警察、出租车司机或类似的人，没有什么东西会来到你的门口。但在这里，东西真的会来到你的门口。

00:29:12.260 --> 00:29:27.760
<v Beachcomber>I have a houseful of found objects, and they get more beautiful all the time. And every day I pick up something. All kind of shells and driftwood, trees. There's an enormous number of things that nature presents to you.
<v Beachcomber>我有一屋子的发现物，而且它们变得越来越美。每天我都会捡到东西。各种贝壳和浮木、树木。大自然呈现给你的东西极其丰富。

00:29:27.760 --> 00:29:47.730
<v Beachcomber>This coast gives a way of life that is really almost-- this is like a myth to me. If you can imagine living like the 19th century, 18th century. Thoreau could've lived here. Thoreau could still live here. And yet most people in America think that that has gone.
<v Beachcomber>这个海岸提供了一种生活方式，这对我来说简直就像个神话。如果你能想象生活在19世纪、18世纪。梭罗本可以住在这里。梭罗仍然可以住在这里。然而大多数美国人认为那种生活已经消失了。

00:29:47.730 --> 00:29:54.790
<v Beachcomber>And yet we're in a time warp here. Temporarily, because as you see, it's going. It's going hourly right now.
<v Beachcomber>然而我们在这里处于一个时间扭曲中。暂时是这样，因为如你所见，它正在消失。现在每小时都在消失。

00:29:54.790 --> 00:30:00.920
<v Beachcomber>[OCEAN WAVES]
<v Beachcomber>[海浪声]

00:30:00.920 --> 00:30:40.160
<v Ira Glass>An excerpt from Ocean Hour in 1979. A link to that entire program is at our website, ThisAmericanLife.org.
<v Ira Glass>这是1979年《海洋时刻》的节选。完整节目的链接在我们网站ThisAmericanLife.org上。

00:30:40.160 --> 00:30:53.440
<v Ira Glass>Coming up, David Sedaris and his family go to the beach. Except for one of them. That's in a minute from Chicago Public Radio and Public Radio International, when our program continues.
<v Ira Glass>接下来，大卫·塞达里斯和他的家人去海滩。除了其中一个。一分钟后，从芝加哥公共广播电台和国际公共广播电台，当我们的节目继续。

00:30:53.440 --> 00:31:09.410
<v Ira Glass>It's This American Life. I'm Ira Glass. Today after these awful weeks where we have all had to learn the phrase "polar vortex" to describe daily life in the United States, we have decided that we're going to spend an hour getting away from all that with stories of places where it is hot, with stories from the beach.
<v Ira Glass>这是《美国生活》。我是艾拉·格拉斯。今天，在经历了这些可怕的几周，我们都不得不学习“极地漩涡”这个词来描述美国日常生活之后，我们决定花一个小时远离这一切，去听听关于那些炎热地方的故事，听听来自海滩的故事。

00:31:09.410 --> 00:31:14.440
<v Ira Glass>We've arrived at Act Four of our show. Act Four, "Now We Are Five."
<v Ira Glass>我们来到了节目的第四幕。第四幕，“现在我们是五个人”。

00:31:14.440 --> 00:31:21.310
<v Ira Glass>OK, if you're a beach family, you go to the beach every year, even bad years for your family. That is just what you do.
<v Ira Glass>好的，如果你是一个海滩家庭，你每年都会去海滩，即使对你的家庭来说是不幸的年份。这就是你们会做的事情。

00:31:21.310 --> 00:31:36.360
<v Ira Glass>David Sedaris has a story about that happening in his family. The story was recently published in The New Yorker . I'm guessing that some of you may have read it. Here at our show, we really loved this story and know lots of you have not seen it, so here it is. Here's David Sedaris.
<v Ira Glass>大卫·塞达里斯有一个关于他家里发生这种情况的故事。这个故事最近发表在《纽约客》上。我猜你们中的一些人可能读过。在我们的节目里，我们非常喜欢这个故事，并且知道你们中的许多人还没看过，所以这就是它。这就是大卫·塞达里斯。

00:31:36.360 --> 00:31:56.730
<v David Sedaris>In late May of last year, a few weeks shy of her 50th birthday, my youngest sister Tiffany committed suicide. She was living in a room in a beat-up house on the hard side of Somerville, Massachusetts, and had been dead, the coroner guessed, for at least five days before her door was battered down.
<v David Sedaris>去年5月底，离我最小的妹妹蒂芙尼50岁生日还有几周时，她自杀身亡了。她住在马萨诸塞州萨默维尔市艰难地带一栋破旧房子的房间里，法医猜测她已经去世至少五天了，直到她的门被撞开。

00:31:56.730 --> 00:32:19.570
<v David Sedaris>I was given the news over a white courtesy phone while at the Dallas airport. Then, because my plane to Baton Rouge was boarding and I wasn't sure what else to do, I got on it. The following morning, I boarded another plane, this one to Atlanta. And the day after that, I flew to Nashville, thinking all the while about my ever shrinking family.
<v David Sedaris>我在达拉斯机场用一台免费电话得到了这个消息。然后，因为我飞往巴吞鲁日的飞机正在登机，我不知道该做什么，就登上了飞机。第二天早上，我又登上了另一架飞往亚特兰大的飞机。在那之后的一天，我飞往纳什维尔，一直在思考我那不断缩小的家庭。

00:32:19.570 --> 00:32:33.870
<v David Sedaris>A person expects his parents to die. But a sibling? I felt I'd lost the identity I'd enjoyed since 1968, when my brother was born. "Six kids?" people would say. "How do your poor folks manage?"
<v David Sedaris>一个人会预料到父母会去世。但兄弟姐妹呢？我觉得我失去了自1968年我弟弟出生以来我所享受的身份。“六个孩子？”人们会说。“你们贫穷的父母是怎么管理的？”

00:32:33.870 --> 00:32:50.320
<v David Sedaris>There were a lot of big families in the neighborhood I grew up in. Every other house was a fiefdom, so I never gave it much thought until I became an adult and my friends started having children. One or two seemed reasonable, but anything beyond that struck me as outrageous.
<v David Sedaris>我长大的邻里有很多大家庭。每隔一户人家就是一个封地，所以我从未太在意，直到我成年，朋友们开始有了孩子。一两个似乎很合理，但超过这个数字让我觉得不可思议。

00:32:50.320 --> 00:33:07.390
<v David Sedaris>A couple Hugh and I knew in Normandy would occasionally come together with their wrecking crew of three. When they'd leave several hours later, every last part of me would feel violated. Take those kids, double them, and subtract the cable TV. That's what my parents had to deal with.
<v David Sedaris>我和休在诺曼底认识的一对夫妇偶尔会带着他们那三个破坏狂孩子一起出现。当他们几个小时后离开时，我身体的每一个部分都感到被侵犯。把那些孩子翻倍，再减去有线电视。这就是我父母必须面对的。

00:33:07.390 --> 00:33:17.270
<v David Sedaris>Now though, there weren't six. Only five. "And you can't really say there used to be six," I told my sister Lisa. "It just makes people uncomfortable."
<v David Sedaris>不过现在，已经没有六个了。只有五个。“而且你不能真的说曾经有六个，”我告诉我的妹妹丽莎。“这只会让人感到不舒服。”

00:33:17.270 --> 00:33:32.760
<v David Sedaris>I recalled a father and son I'd met in California a few years back. "So are there other children?" I asked. "There are," the man said. "Three who are living, and a daughter-- Chloe-- who died before she was born 18 years ago."
<v David Sedaris>我想起了几年前我在加州遇到的一对父子。“那么还有其他孩子吗？”我问。“有，”那个人说。“三个活着的，还有一个女儿——克洛伊——在18年前出生前就去世了。”

00:33:32.760 --> 00:33:47.870
<v David Sedaris>That's not fair, I remember thinking. Because, I mean, what's a person supposed to do with that?
<v David Sedaris>那不公平，我记得当时心想。因为，我的意思是，一个人该拿那个怎么办？

00:33:47.870 --> 00:34:07.810
<v David Sedaris>Compared to most 49 year-olds, or even most 49 month-olds, Tiffany didn't have much. She did leave a will though. In it she decreed that we, her family, could not have her body or attend her memorial service. "So put that in your pipe and smoke it!" our mother would've said.
<v David Sedaris>与大多数49岁的人，甚至大多数49个月大的人相比，蒂芙尼没有多少东西。但她确实留下了遗嘱。在遗嘱中，她规定我们，她的家人，不能拥有她的尸体或参加她的追悼会。“那么把你这套留着自己抽吧！”我母亲会说。

00:34:07.810 --> 00:34:32.090
<v David Sedaris>A few days after getting the news, my sister Amy drove to Somerville with a friend and collected two boxes of things from Tiffany's room. Family photos-- many of which had been ripped into pieces-- comment cards from a neighborhood grocery store, notebooks, receipts. The bed, a mattress on the floor, had been taken away and a large industrial fan had been set up.
<v David Sedaris>在得到消息几天后，我妹妹艾米带着一个朋友开车去了萨默维尔，从蒂芙尼的房间里收集了两箱东西。家庭照片——许多已经被撕成了碎片——社区杂货店的评论卡、笔记本、收据。那张在地板上的床垫已经被拿走了，并安装了一台巨大的工业风扇。

00:34:32.090 --> 00:34:40.040
<v David Sedaris>Amy snapped some pictures while she was there. And individually and in groups, those of us left studied them for clues.
<v David Sedaris>艾米在那里拍了一些照片。我们剩下的人单独或成群结队地研究它们寻找线索。

00:34:40.040 --> 00:34:55.040
<v David Sedaris>A paper plate on a dresser that had several drawers missing. A phone number written on a wall. A collection of mop handles, each one a different color, arranged like cattails in a barrel painted green.
<v David Sedaris>梳妆台上一个少了几个抽屉的纸盘子。墙上写着一个电话号码。一堆拖把柄，每一个颜色都不同，像桶里的香蒲一样排列着，漆成了绿色。

00:34:55.040 --> 00:35:16.930
<v David Sedaris>Six months before our sister killed herself, I'd made plans for us all to gather at a beach house on Emerald Isle, off the coast of North Carolina. My family used to vacation there every summer, but after my mother died we stopped going. Not because we lost interest, but because it was she who always made the arrangements and more importantly, paid for it.
<v David Sedaris>在我们妹妹自杀六个月前，我已经计划我们全家人聚集在北卡罗来纳州海岸外的翡翠岛的一栋海滩别墅里。我家人过去每年夏天都在那里度假，但母亲去世后我们就没再去了。不是因为我们失去了兴趣，而是因为总是她负责安排，更重要的是，支付费用。

00:35:16.930 --> 00:35:45.820
<v David Sedaris>The place I found, with the help of my sister-in-law Kathy, had six bedrooms and a small swimming pool. Our week long rental period began on Saturday, June 8th, and we arrived to find a delivery woman standing in the driveway with seven pounds of seafood, a sympathy gift sent by friends. "They's slaw in there too," she said, handing over the bags.
<v David Sedaris>在我嫂子凯茜的帮助下，我找到的那个地方有六间卧室和一个小游泳池。我们为期一周的租赁期从6月8日星期六开始，我们到达时发现一位送货员站在车道上，带着七磅海鲜，这是朋友们送来的慰问礼物。“里面还有凉拌卷心菜，”她说，递过袋子。

00:35:45.820 --> 00:35:56.810
<v David Sedaris>In the past, when my family rented a cottage, my sisters and I would crowd the door like puppies around a food dish. Our father would unlock it and we'd tear through the house claiming rooms.
<v David Sedaris>过去，当我家人租小屋时，我的姐妹们和我会像食物盘周围的小狗一样挤在门口。我父亲会打开门，我们会冲进房子抢房间。

00:35:56.810 --> 00:36:20.400
<v David Sedaris>I always picked the biggest one facing the ocean. And just as I'd start to unpack, my parents would enter and tell me that this was theirs. "I mean, just who the hell do you think you are?" my father would ask. He and my mother would move in and I would get booted to what was called the maid's room. It was always on the ground level, a kind of dank shed next to where the car was parked.
<v David Sedaris>我总是挑选最大的一间面向大海的。正当我开始拆包时，父母会进来告诉我那是他们的。“我的意思是，你以为你自己是谁？”我父亲会问。他和母亲会搬进去，而我会被踢到所谓的佣人房。它总是在底层，一种停放汽车旁边的潮湿棚屋。

00:36:20.400 --> 00:36:33.700
<v David Sedaris>There was never an interior stairway leading to the upper floor. Instead I had to take the outside steps, and more often than not, knock on the locked front door like a beggar hoping to be invited in.
<v David Sedaris>从来没有通往楼上的内部楼梯。相反，我必须走外面的台阶，而且通常情况下，像个乞丐一样敲锁住的前门，希望能被邀请进去。

00:36:33.700 --> 00:36:40.060
<v David Sedaris>"What do you want?" my sisters would ask. "I want to come inside."
<v David Sedaris>“你想要什么？”我的姐妹们会问。“我想进来。”

00:36:40.060 --> 00:36:54.030
<v David Sedaris>"That's funny," Lisa, the eldest, would say to the others who were gathered like disciples around her. "Did you hear something, a whining sound? What is it that makes a noise like that? A hermit crab? A little sea slug?"
<v David Sedaris>“那很有趣，”丽莎，最年长的，会对比她周围像门徒一样聚集的人说。“你们听到了吗，一种呜呜声？是什么东西发出了那样的声音？寄居蟹？小海蛞蝓？”

00:36:54.030 --> 00:37:13.330
<v David Sedaris>Normally there was a social divide between the three oldest and three youngest children in my family. Lisa, Gretchen, and I treated the others like servants and did very well for ourselves. At the beach though, all bets were off. And it was just upstairs against downstairs, meaning everyone against me.
<v David Sedaris>通常我家里最大的三个孩子和最小的三个孩子之间有社会鸿沟。丽莎、格雷琴和我把其他人当作仆人，我们自己做得很好。但在海滩上，一切都不好说了。那就是楼上对抗楼下，意味着每个人都对抗我。

00:37:13.330 --> 00:37:29.500
<v David Sedaris>This time, because I was paying, I got to choose the best room. Amy moved in next door, and my brother Paul, his wife, and their 10-year-old daughter Maddie took the spot next to her. That was it for oceanfront. The others arrived later and had to take the leftovers.
<v David Sedaris>这次，因为是我付钱，我得以选择最好的房间。艾米搬到隔壁，我弟弟保罗、他的妻子和他们10岁的女儿麦迪搬进了旁边的位置。海滨就这些了。其他人后来才到，不得不捡剩下的。

00:37:29.500 --> 00:37:46.430
<v David Sedaris>Lisa's room faced the street, as did my father's. Gretchen's faced the street and was intended for someone who was paralyzed. Hanging from the ceiling were electric pulleys designed to lift a harnessed body into and out of bed.
<v David Sedaris>丽莎的房间面向街道，我父亲的也是。格雷琴的面向街道，是为瘫痪的人准备的。天花板上悬挂着电动滑轮，旨在将身体系上安全带抬进或抬出床。

00:37:46.430 --> 00:37:54.860
<v David Sedaris>Unlike the cottages of our youth, this one did not have a maid's room. It was too new and fancy for that, as were the homes that surrounded it.
<v David Sedaris>与我们年轻时的小屋不同，这栋房子没有佣人房。它对那种设施来说太新太豪华了，周围的房子也一样。

00:37:54.860 --> 00:38:13.420
<v David Sedaris>Traditionally, all the island houses were on stilts. But more and more often now, the ground floors are filled in. They all have beachy names and are painted beachy colors. But most of those built after Hurricane Fran hit the coast in 1996 are three stories tall and look almost suburban.
<v David Sedaris>传统上，岛上的房子都在高跷上。但现在越来越多的底层被填满了。它们都有海滩的名字，涂着海滩的颜色。但大多数在1996年弗兰飓风袭击海岸后建造的房子都有三层楼高，看起来几乎像郊区住宅。

00:38:13.420 --> 00:38:21.360
<v David Sedaris>This place was vast and airy. The kitchen table sat 12. And there was not one, but two dishwashers.
<v David Sedaris>这个地方广阔而通风。餐桌能坐下12个人。而且不只一个，而是两个洗碗机。

00:38:21.360 --> 00:38:37.000
<v David Sedaris>All the pictures were ocean related. Seascapes and lighthouses, all with the airborne Vs that are shorthand for seagull. A sampler on the living room wall read "Old shellers never die, they simply conch out."
<v David Sedaris>所有的画都与海洋有关。海景和灯塔，全都有象征海鸥的空中的V字。客厅墙上的一块采样画写着“老贝壳收藏者永远不会死，他们只是没法再捡了。”

00:38:37.000 --> 00:38:58.380
<v David Sedaris>On the round clock beside it, the numbers lay in an indecipherable heap, as if they'd come unglued. Just above them were printed the words "Who cares?" This was what we found ourselves saying whenever anyone asked the time. "Who cares?"
<v David Sedaris>旁边圆钟上的数字乱作一团，好像脱胶了一样。就在它们上方印刷着“谁在乎？”这正是我们发现自己无论何时有人问时间时都会说的话。“谁在乎？”

00:38:58.380 --> 00:39:16.760
<v David Sedaris>The day before we arrived at the beach, Tiffany's obituary ran in the Raleigh News & Observer. It was submitted by Gretchen, who stated that our sister had passed away peacefully at her home. This made it sound as if she were very old and had a house. But what else could you do?
<v David Sedaris>我们到达海滩的前一天，蒂芙尼的讣告刊登在《罗利新闻与观察家报》上。是由格雷琴提交的，她声明我们妹妹在家里平静地去世了。这听起来好像她很老而且有房子。但还能做什么呢？

00:39:16.760 --> 00:39:41.040
<v David Sedaris>People were leaving responses on the paper's website, and one fellow wrote that Tiffany used to come into the video store where he worked in Somerville. When his glasses broke, she offered him a pair she had found while foraging for art supplies in somebody's trashcan. He said she also gave him a Playboy magazine from the 1960s that included a photo spread titled "The Ass Menagerie."
<v David Sedaris>人们在报纸网站上留下回复，一个人写道，蒂芙尼过去常去他在萨默维尔工作的视频店。当他的眼镜碎了时，她给了他一副她从某人的垃圾桶里寻找艺术品时发现的眼镜。他说她还给了他一本1960年代的《花花公子》杂志，其中包括名为“驴子动物园”的照片专栏。

00:39:41.040 --> 00:39:57.540
<v David Sedaris>This was fascinating, as we didn't really know our sister very well. Each of us had pulled away from the family at some point in our lives. We'd had to, in order to forge our own identities, to go from being a Sedaris to our own specific Sedaris.
<v David Sedaris>这很有趣，因为我们并不真正了解我们的妹妹。我们每个人都在生命的某个时刻远离过家庭。我们不得不这样做，为了打造我们自己的身份，从一个塞达里斯变成我们自己特定的塞达里斯。

00:39:57.540 --> 00:40:09.550
<v David Sedaris>Tiffany though, stayed away. She might promise to come home for Christmas, but at the last minute there'd always be some excuse. She missed her plane, she had to work. The same would happen with our summer vacations.
<v David Sedaris>但蒂芙尼一直远离。她可能会承诺回家过圣诞节，但最后一刻总会有各种借口。她错过了飞机，她必须工作。我们的暑假也会发生同样的事情。

00:40:09.550 --> 00:40:20.660
<v David Sedaris>"The rest of us managed to make it," I'd say, aware of how old and guilt trippy I sounded. All of us would be disappointed by her absence, though for different reasons.
<v David Sedaris>“我们其他人都能做到，”我会说，意识到我听起来有多老和多么利用负罪感。我们所有人都会因为她的缺席而失望，尽管原因各不相同。

00:40:20.660 --> 00:40:44.800
<v David Sedaris>Even if you weren't getting along with Tiffany at the time, you couldn't deny the show she put on. The dramatic entrances, the nonstop professional grade insults, the chaos she'd inevitably leave in her wake. One day she'd throw a dish at you and the next she'd create a mosaic made of the shards. When allegiances with one brother or sister flamed out, she'd take up with someone else.
<v David Sedaris>即使你当时和蒂芙尼相处得不好，你也无法否认她展示出的表演。戏剧性的入场，不停歇的专业级侮辱，她不可避免地会在身后留下的混乱。今天她会朝你扔盘子，明天她会用碎片制作马赛克。当与一个兄弟姐妹的联盟破裂时，她会和另一个人在一起。

00:40:44.800 --> 00:41:10.720
<v David Sedaris>At no time did she get along with everybody, but there was always someone she was in contact with. Toward the end, it was Lisa. But before that, we'd all had our turn. The last time she joined us on Emerald Isle was 1986. "And even then, she left after three days," Gretchen reminded us.
<v David Sedaris>她从未在任何时候与每个人都相处得好，但总有某个人是她保持联系的。快到最后时，是丽莎。但在那之前，我们都轮到过。她最后一次在翡翠岛加入我们是1986年。“即便那时，她三天后就离开了，”格雷琴提醒我们。

00:41:10.720 --> 00:41:18.490
<v David Sedaris>As kids, we spent our beach time swimming. Then we became teenagers and devoted ourselves to tanning.
<v David Sedaris>作为孩子，我们花在海滩上的时间就是游泳。然后我们成了青少年，致力于晒黑。

00:41:18.490 --> 00:41:35.140
<v David Sedaris>There's a certain kind of talk that takes place when you're lying dazed in the sun, and I've always been partial to it. On the first afternoon of our most recent trip, we laid out one of the bedspreads we'd had as children and arranged ourselves side by side on it, trading stories about Tiffany.
<v David Sedaris>当你神志不清地躺在阳光下时，会发生某种交谈，我一直很喜欢这种交谈。在我们最近一次旅行的第一个下午，我们铺开了一条我们小时候用过的床罩，并排躺在上面，交换关于蒂芙尼的故事。

00:41:35.140 --> 00:41:42.580
<v David Sedaris>"What about the Halloween she spent on that Army base? And the time she showed up at Dad's birthday party with a black eye."
<v David Sedaris>“那年万圣节她在军队基地度过记得吗？还有她带着黑眼圈出现在爸爸生日派对上的时候。”

00:41:42.580 --> 00:42:00.440
<v David Sedaris>"I remember this girl she met years ago at a party," I began when my turn came. "She'd been talking about facial scars and how terrible it would be to have one. So Tiffany said, 'I have a little scar on my face and I don't think it's so awful.' 'Well,' the girl said, 'you would if you were pretty.'"
<v David Sedaris>“我记得这个女孩，她几年前在派对上认识的，”轮到我时我开始说。“她在谈论面部疤痕以及脸上有一道疤痕有多可怕。所以蒂芙尼说，‘我脸上有一道小疤痕，我不觉得有什么可怕的。’‘嗯，’那个女孩说，‘如果你长得漂亮，你就会这么觉得了。’”

00:42:00.440 --> 00:42:05.100
<v David Sedaris>Amy laughed and rolled over onto her stomach. "Oh, that's a good line."
<v David Sedaris>艾米笑了，翻身趴着。“哦，那是一句好台词。”

00:42:05.100 --> 00:42:09.740
<v David Sedaris>I rearranged the towel I was using as a pillow. "Idn't it, though?"
<v David Sedaris>我重新调整了用作枕头的毛巾。“难道不是吗？”

00:42:09.740 --> 00:42:33.570
<v David Sedaris>Coming from someone else, the story might have been upsetting. But not being pretty was never one of Tiffany's problems, especially when she was in her 20s and 30s and men tumbled helpless before her. "Funny," I said, "but I don't remember a scar on her face."
<v David Sedaris>如果来自别人，这个故事可能会令人不安。但不够漂亮从来不是蒂芙尼的问题，尤其是当她在20多岁和30多岁时，男人们在她面前无助地摔倒。“有趣的是，”我说，“但我记不起她脸上有疤痕。”

00:42:33.570 --> 00:42:52.830
<v David Sedaris>I stayed in the sun too long that day and got a burn on my forehead. That was basically it for me and the beach blanket. I made brief appearances for the rest of the week, stopping to dry off after a swim. But mainly I spent my days on a bike, cycling up and down the coast and thinking about what had happened.
<v David Sedaris>那天我在阳光下待得太久，前额被晒伤了。对于我和海滩毯来说，基本上就这样了。这一周剩下的时间里我只短暂地出现，游泳后停下来擦干。但主要是白天骑自行车，沿着海岸骑行，思考发生了什么。

00:42:52.830 --> 00:43:08.140
<v David Sedaris>While the rest of us seemed to get along effortlessly, with Tiffany it always felt like work. She and I usually made up after arguing, but our last fight took it out of me. And at the time of her death, we hadn't spoken in eight years.
<v David Sedaris>虽然我们其他人似乎不费吹灰之力就相处得很好，但对于蒂芙尼，总是感觉像是工作。她和我在争吵后通常会和好，但我们的最后一次争吵让我耗尽了精力。在她去世时，我们已经八年没说话了。

00:43:08.140 --> 00:43:31.550
<v David Sedaris>During that period, I regularly found myself near Somerville. And though I'd always toy with the idea of contacting her, I never did, despite my father's encouragement. Meanwhile, I'd get reports from him and Lisa. Tiffany had lost her apartment, had gone on disability, had moved into a room found for her by a social service agency.
<v David Sedaris>在那期间，我经常发现自己在萨默维尔附近。尽管我总是考虑联系她，但我从没这样做，尽管我父亲鼓励我。同时，我会从他和丽莎那里得到报告。蒂芙尼失去了公寓，靠残疾金生活，搬进了一个社会服务机构为她找到的房间。

00:43:31.550 --> 00:44:02.770
<v David Sedaris>Perhaps she was more forthcoming with her friends, but her family got things only in bits and pieces. She didn't talk with us so much as at us. Great blocks of speech that were in turns funny, astute, and so contradictory it was hard to connect the sentence you were hearing to the one that preceded it. Before we stopped speaking, I could always tell when she was on the phone. I'd walk into the house and hear Hugh say, "Uh huh, uh huh, uh huh."
<v David Sedaris>也许她和朋友们更坦率，但她的家人只得到了零星的碎片。她不是和我们说话，而是对我们大喊。一大段一大段的谈话，有时很有趣、精明，而且自相矛盾到很难把你听到的句子和你刚才听到的那句联系起来。在我们停止交谈之前，我总能知道她在打电话。我会走进屋子听休说，“嗯哼，嗯哼，嗯哼。”

00:44:02.770 --> 00:44:19.010
<v David Sedaris>In addition to the two boxes that Amy had filled in Somerville, she also brought down our sister's 1978 ninth grade yearbook. Among the messages inscribed by her classmates was the following, written by someone who had drawn a marijuana leaf beside her name.
<v David Sedaris>除了艾米在萨默维尔装满的那两箱东西外，她还带下了我们妹妹1978年九年级的年鉴。在同学们书写的留言中，有下面这一条，是由一个在她名字旁边画了大麻叶的人写的。

00:44:19.010 --> 00:44:35.880
<v David Sedaris>"Tiffany, you are a one of a kind girl, so stay that way, you unique ass. I'm only sorry we couldn't have partied more together. This school sucks to hell. Stay cool, stoned, drunk, [BLEEP] up. Check your ass later."
<v David Sedaris>“蒂芙尼，你是一个独一无二的女孩，所以保持那样，你这个独特的混蛋。我只遗憾我们没能多一起聚聚。这学校烂透了。保持酷，嗑药，喝醉，[哔] 搞砸了。回头再来找你。”

00:44:35.880 --> 00:44:43.930
<v David Sedaris>Then there's, "Tiffany, I'm looking forward to getting high with you this summer." And "Tiffany, call me some time this summer and we'll go out and get blitzed."
<v David Sedaris>然后还有，“蒂芙尼，我很期待这个夏天和你一起嗨。”以及“蒂芙尼，这个夏天什么时候给我打电话，我们一起出去喝个酩酊大醉。”

00:44:43.930 --> 00:45:08.180
<v David Sedaris>A few weeks after these messages were written, Tiffany ran away and was subsequently sent to a disciplinary institution in Maine called Elan. According to what she told us later, it was a horrible place. She returned home in 1980, having spent two years there. And from that point on, none of us can recall a conversation in which she did not mention it.
<v David Sedaris>在这些留言写下几周后，蒂芙尼逃跑了，随后被送到缅因州一个名为Elan的纪律机构。据她后来告诉我们的，那是一个可怕的地方。她在1980年回到家，在那里待了两年。从那时起，我们没人能回忆起她没有提到过那个地方的谈话。

00:45:08.180 --> 00:45:18.830
<v David Sedaris>She blamed the family for sending her off. But we, her siblings, had nothing to do with it. Paul for instance. Paul was 10 when she left. I was 21.
<v David Sedaris>她责怪家人把她送走。但我们，她的兄弟姐妹，与此无关。比如保罗。她离开时保罗才10岁。我21岁。

00:45:18.830 --> 00:45:24.640
<v David Sedaris>For a year I sent her monthly letters. Then she wrote and told me to stop.
<v David Sedaris>一年里我每月给她写信。然后她写信让我停止。

00:45:24.640 --> 00:45:43.880
<v David Sedaris>As for my parents, there were only so many times they could apologize. "We had other kids," they said in their defense. "You think we could let the world stop on account of any one of you?"
<v David Sedaris>至于我的父母，他们道歉的次数太多了。“我们还有其他孩子，”他们辩护说。“你以为为了你们中的任何一个，我们能让世界停下来吗？”

00:45:43.880 --> 00:46:08.750
<v David Sedaris>We were at the beach  for three days before Lisa and our father-- who is now 90-- joined us. Being on the island meant missing the spinning classes he takes in Raleigh, so I found a fitness center not far from the rental cottage and every afternoon he and I would spend some time there. On the way over, we'd talk to each other. But as soon as we mounted our stationary bikes, we'd each retreat into our own thoughts.
<v David Sedaris>我们在海滩待了三天后，丽莎和我们现在90岁的父亲才加入我们。在岛上意味着错过他在罗利参加的动感单车课，所以我找到了离租赁小屋不远的一个健身中心，每天下午他和我会花点时间在那里。去的那路上，我们会交谈。但一旦我们骑上固定自行车，我们就会各自退回到自己的思绪中。

00:46:08.750 --> 00:46:27.900
<v David Sedaris>It was a small place, not very lively. A mute television oversaw the room, tuned to the Weather Channel and reminding us that there's always a catastrophe somewhere or other, always someone flooded from his home, or running for his life from a funnel shaped cloud.
<v David Sedaris>那是个小地方，不太热闹。一台静音电视机监视着房间，调到了天气频道，提醒我们某个地方总有灾难，总有人被洪水淹没了家园，或从漏斗状云层下逃命。

00:46:27.900 --> 00:46:51.920
<v David Sedaris>Toward the end of the week, I came upon my father in Amy's room, sifting through the photos that Tiffany had destroyed. In his hand was a fragment of my mother's head with a patch of blue sky behind her. Under what circumstances had this been ripped up, I wondered. It seemed such a melodramatic gesture, like throwing a glass against a wall. Something someone in a movie would do.
<v David Sedaris>一周快结束时，我在艾米的房间里遇到了我父亲，他在翻看蒂芙尼毁掉的照片。他手里拿着我母亲头部的一个碎片，背后是一片蓝天。我不禁想，在什么情况下这被撕碎了？这似乎是一种戏剧性的姿态，就像把玻璃杯扔在墙上。电影里的人会做的事情。

00:46:51.920 --> 00:47:01.730
<v David Sedaris>"Just awful," my father whispered. "A person's life reduced to one lousy box."
<v David Sedaris>“真是太糟糕了，”我父亲低声说。“一个人的生活被缩减成一个烂箱子。”

00:47:01.730 --> 00:47:08.760
<v David Sedaris>I put my hand on his shoulder. [CLEARS  THROAT] "Actually, there are two of them."
<v David Sedaris>我把手放在他的肩膀上。[清嗓子] “实际上，有两个。”

00:47:08.760 --> 00:47:14.840
<v David Sedaris>He corrected himself. "Two lousy boxes."
<v David Sedaris>他纠正了自己。“两个烂箱子。”

00:47:14.840 --> 00:47:30.530
<v David Sedaris>One afternoon on Emerald Isle, we all rode to the Food Lion for groceries. I was in the produce department looking at red onions when my brother sneaked up from behind and let loose with a load "Achoo!" This while whipping a bouquet of wet parsley through the air.
<v David Sedaris>翡翠岛的一个下午，我们全家骑车去Food Lion超市买杂货。我在农产品部门看红洋葱时，我弟弟从背后溜过来，大声打了一个“阿嚏！”同时把一束湿欧芹甩向空中。

00:47:30.530 --> 00:47:49.490
<v David Sedaris>I felt the spray on the back of my neck and froze, thinking a very sick stranger had just sneezed on me. It's a neat trick, but he also dowsed the Indian woman who was standing to my left. She was wearing a blood-colored sari, so got it on her bare arm as well as her neck and the lower part of her back.
<v David Sedaris>我感觉到脖子后面有喷雾，僵住了，以为一个非常生病的陌生人刚打喷嚏在我身上。这是一个巧妙的把戏，但他还把站在我左边的印度女人泼湿了。她穿着血色的纱丽，所以她的裸露手臂以及脖子和背部下半部分都沾上了。

00:47:49.490 --> 00:47:55.710
<v David Sedaris>"Sorry, man!" Paul said when she turned around, horrified. "I was just playing a joke on my brother!"
<v David Sedaris>“抱歉，伙计！”那个女人惊恐地转过身时，保罗说。“我只是在跟我哥哥开个玩笑！”

00:47:55.710 --> 00:48:06.310
<v David Sedaris>The woman had many thin bracelets on and they jangled as she brushed her hand against the back of her head. "You called her man," I said to him after she walked off.
<v David Sedaris>那个女人戴着许多细手镯，当她把手扫过头后时，手镯发出叮当声。“你叫她伙计，”在她走开后我对他说道。

00:48:06.310 --> 00:48:12.030
<v David Sedaris>"For real?" he asked. Amy mimicked him perfectly. "For real?"
<v David Sedaris>“真的吗？”他问。艾米完美地模仿了他。“真的吗？”

00:48:12.030 --> 00:48:26.310
<v David Sedaris>Over the phone, my brother, like me, is often mistaken for a woman. As we continued shopping, he told us that his van had recently broken down. And that when he called for a tow truck, the dispatcher said, "We'll be right out, sweetie!"
<v David Sedaris>在电话里，我弟弟像我一样，经常被误认为女人。当我们继续购物时，他告诉我们他的货车最近抛锚了。当他叫拖车时，调度员说，“我们马上就到，甜心！”

00:48:26.310 --> 00:48:51.540
<v David Sedaris>He lowered a watermelon into the cart and turned to his daughter. "Maddie's got a daddy who talks like a lady, but she don't care, do she?" Giggling, she punched him in the stomach, and I was struck by how comfortable the two of them are with each other. Our father was a figure of authority, but Paul is more of a playmate.
<v David Sedaris>他把一个西瓜放进购物车，转向他的女儿。“麦迪有一个说话像女士的爸爸，但她不在乎，对吧？”咯咯笑着，她打了他肚子一下，我被他们俩相处得多么自在所打动。我们的父亲是权威形象，但保罗更像个玩伴。

00:48:51.540 --> 00:49:03.740
<v David Sedaris>When we went to the beach as children, on or about the fourth day, our father would say, "Wouldn't it be nice to buy a cottage down here?" We'd get our hopes up and then he would bring practical concerns into it.
<v David Sedaris>当我们小时候去海滩时，在第四天左右，我父亲会说，“在这里买个小屋不是很好吗？”我们会充满希望，然后他会提出实际的顾虑。

00:49:03.740 --> 00:49:14.310
<v David Sedaris>They weren't petty. Buying a house that will eventually get blown away by a hurricane probably isn't the best way to spend your money. But still, we wanted one desperately.
<v David Sedaris>它们并不琐碎。购买一栋最终会被飓风吹走的房子可能不是花钱的最佳方式。但我们仍然非常渴望要一个。

00:49:14.310 --> 00:49:24.800
<v David Sedaris>I told myself when I was young that one day I would buy a beach house and then it would be everyone's. As long as they followed my draconian rules and never stopped thanking me for it.
<v David Sedaris>我年轻时告诉自己，有一天我会买一栋海滩别墅，然后它会成为每个人的。只要他们遵守我严苛的规则，并且永远不要停止因为这个而感谢我。

00:49:24.800 --> 00:49:44.650
<v David Sedaris>Thus it was on Wednesday morning midway through our vacation Hugh and I contacted a real estate agent named Phyllis, who took us around to look at available properties. On Friday afternoon, we made an offer on an oceanfront cottage not far from the one we were renting. And before sunset, our bid was accepted.
<v David Sedaris>于是，在我们假期中间的星期三上午，休和我联系了一位名叫菲莉丝的房地产经纪人，她带我们去看可用的房产。星期五下午，我们对离我们租的小屋不远的一栋海滨小屋出价了。日落之前，我们的报价被接受了。

00:49:44.650 --> 00:49:53.510
<v David Sedaris>I made the announcement at the dinner table and got the reaction I had expected. "Now wait a minute," my father said. "You need to think clearly here!"
<v David Sedaris>我在餐桌上宣布了这个消息，并得到了我预期的反应。“现在等一下，”我父亲说。“你需要清楚地考虑一下！”

00:49:53.510 --> 00:49:59.850
<v David Sedaris>"I already have," I told him. "OK then, how old is the roof? How many times has it been replaced in the past 10 years?"
<v David Sedaris>“我已经考虑清楚了，”我告诉他。“那么，屋顶有多大年纪了？过去10年换过几次？”

00:49:59.850 --> 00:50:08.630
<v David Sedaris>"When can we move in?" Gretchen asked. Lisa wanted to know if she could bring her dogs, and Amy asked what the house was named.
<v David Sedaris>“我们可以什么时候搬进去？”格雷琴问。丽莎想知道她能不能带她的狗，艾米问房子叫什么名字。

00:50:08.630 --> 00:50:14.160
<v David Sedaris>"Right now, it's called Fantastic Place," I told her. "But we're going to change it."
<v David Sedaris>“现在它叫梦幻之地，”我告诉她。“但我们要改名字。”

00:50:14.160 --> 00:50:27.690
<v David Sedaris>I used to think the ideal name for a beach house was the Ship Shape. Now though, I had a better idea. "We're going to call it The Sea Section."
<v David Sedaris>我过去认为海滩别墅的理想名字是“船形”。但现在我有了一个更好的主意。“我们要把它叫作‘海区’。”

00:50:27.690 --> 00:50:30.800
<v David Sedaris>My father put down his hamburger. "Oh no you're not!"
<v David Sedaris>我父亲放下了汉堡。“噢不，你不能！”

00:50:30.800 --> 00:50:42.930
<v David Sedaris>"But it's perfect!" I argued. "The name's supposed to be beachy, and if it's a pun, all the better." I brought up a cottage we'd seen earlier in the day called Dune Our Thing, and my father winced.
<v David Sedaris>“但它很完美！”我争辩道。“名字应该是海滩风格的，如果是双关语，那就更好了。”我提到了我们当天早些时候看到的一栋名为“筑我们的事”的小屋，我父亲畏缩了一下。

00:50:42.930 --> 00:50:57.690
<v David Sedaris>"What about naming it Tiffany?" he said. Our silence translated to let's pretend we didn't hear that. He picked his hamburger back up. "I think it's a great idea. The perfect way to pay our respects."
<v David Sedaris>“把它命名为蒂芙尼怎么样？”他说。我们的沉默翻译过来就是让我们假装没听到。他重新拿起汉堡。“我觉得这是个好主意。表达我们敬意的完美方式。”

00:50:57.690 --> 00:51:06.000
<v David Sedaris>"If that's the case, we could name it after Mom," I told him. "Or half after Mom and half after Tiffany. But it's a house, not a tombstone, and it wouldn't fit in with the names of the other houses."
<v David Sedaris>“如果是那样的话，我们可以用妈妈的名字命名，”我告诉他。“或者一半以妈妈命名，一半以蒂芙尼命名。但这是一栋房子，不是墓碑，而且它不符合其他房子的名字。”

00:51:06.000 --> 00:51:11.640
<v David Sedaris>"Oh baloney!" my father said. "Fitting in! That's not who we are. That's not what we're about."
<v David Sedaris>“噢胡说！”我父亲说。“合群！那不是我们是谁。那不是我们要谈论的。”

00:51:11.640 --> 00:51:19.940
<v David Sedaris>Paul interrupted to nominate the Conch Sucker. Amy's suggestion had the word seamen in it, and Gretchen's was even dirtier.
<v David Sedaris>保罗打断说要提名“海螺吸吮者”。艾米的建议里有“海员”这个词，而格雷琴的甚至更脏。

00:51:19.940 --> 00:51:29.480
<v David Sedaris>"What's wrong with the name it already has?" Lisa asked. "No, no, no," my father said, forgetting I think, that this wasn't his decision.
<v David Sedaris>“它原来的名字有什么不好？”丽莎问。“不，不，不，”我父亲说，我想他是忘了这根本不是他决定的。

00:51:29.480 --> 00:51:48.770
<v David Sedaris>A few days later, after the buyer's remorse had kicked in, I'd wonder if I hadn't bought the house as a way of saying, see, it's just that easy. No hemming and hawing, no asking to look at the septic tank. Rather you make your family happy and iron out the details later.
<v David Sedaris>几天后，买家后悔的感觉涌上心头，我怀疑我是不是买这栋房子是为了以此说，看，这很容易。没有犹豫不决，没有要求看化粪池。反而是你让你的家人快乐，然后再处理细节。

00:51:48.770 --> 00:52:10.650
<v David Sedaris>The cottage we bought is two stories tall and was built in 1978. It's on proper stilts and has two rear decks, one above the other overlooking the ocean. It was rented to vacationers until late September, but Phyllis allowed us to drop by and show it to the family the following morning after we'd checked out of the house we'd been staying in.
<v David Sedaris>我们买下的小屋有两层，建于1978年。它在高跷上，有两个后甲板，一个在另一个上面，俯瞰着海洋。它租给度假者直到9月底，但菲莉丝允许我们顺便过来，在我们退房后的第二天早上向家人展示。

00:52:10.650 --> 00:52:25.190
<v David Sedaris>A place always looks different-- worse, most often-- after you've made the commitment to buy it. So while the others raced up and down the stairs claiming their future bedrooms, I held my nose to a vent and caught a whiff of mildew.
<v David Sedaris>一栋房子总是看起来不同——通常更糟——在你承诺买下它之后。所以当其他人在楼上楼下跑着争夺他们未来的卧室时，我把鼻子凑近通风口，闻到了一股霉味。

00:52:25.190 --> 00:52:52.870
<v David Sedaris>The sale included the furniture, so I also made an inventory of the Barcaloungers and massive TVs I would eventually be getting rid of, along with the shell patterned bedspreads and cushions with anchors on them. "For our beach house, I want to have a train theme," I announced. "Trains on the curtains, trains on the towels. We're gonna go all out." "Oh brother," my father moaned.
<v David Sedaris>出售包括家具，所以我也列了一份清单，清单上有我最终要处理掉的巴卡躺椅和巨大的电视，还有贝壳图案的床罩和有锚图案的靠垫。“对于我们的海滩别墅，我想有一个火车主题，”我宣布。“窗帘上有火车，毛巾上有火车。我们要全力以赴。” “噢哥哥，”我父亲哀叹道。

00:52:52.870 --> 00:53:13.500
<v David Sedaris>We sketched a plan to return for Thanksgiving. And after saying goodbye to one another, my family splintered into groups and headed off to our respective homes. There'd been a breeze at the beach house, but once we left the island the air grew still. As the heat intensified, so did the general feeling of depression.
<v David Sedaris>我们制定了感恩节回来的计划。道别之后，我的家人分成小组前往各自的家。海滩别墅那边有微风，但一旦离开岛屿，空气就变得静止了。随着热度加剧，抑郁的感觉也随之加剧。

00:53:13.500 --> 00:53:27.460
<v David Sedaris>Throughout the '60s and '70s, the road back to Raleigh took us past Smithfield and a billboard on the outskirts of town that read "Welcome to Klan Country." This time we took a different route, one my brother recommend.
<v David Sedaris>在整个60年代和70年代，回罗利的路经过史密斯菲尔德，镇郊的一块广告牌上写着“欢迎来到三K党国家”。这次我们走了一条不同的路线，我弟弟推荐的那条。

00:53:27.460 --> 00:53:43.090
<v David Sedaris>Hugh drove and my father sat beside him. I slumped down in the back seat next to Amy and every time I raised my head, I'd see the same soybean field or low slung cinder block building we'd seemingly passed 20 minutes earlier.
<v David Sedaris>休开车，我父亲坐在他旁边。我瘫倒在艾米旁边的后座上，每次我抬起头，我看到的似乎都是我们20分钟前刚经过的同一块大豆田或低矮的煤渣块建筑。

00:53:43.090 --> 00:53:59.660
<v David Sedaris>We'd been on the road for a little more than an hour when we stopped at a farmer's market. Inside an open air pavilion, a woman offered complimentary plates of hummus served with a corn and black bean salad, so we each accepted one and took seats on a bench.
<v David Sedaris>我们在路上行驶了一个多小时后，在一个农贸市场停了下来。在一个露天亭子里，一位女士提供了免费的鹰嘴豆泥，配上玉米和黑豆沙拉，所以我们每个人都接受了一份并坐在长椅上。

00:53:59.660 --> 00:54:12.980
<v David Sedaris>20 years earlier, the most a place like this might have offered was fried okra. Now there was organic coffee, and artisanal goat cheese. Above our heads hung a sign that read "Whispering Dove Ranch."
<v David Sedaris>20年前，像这样的地方最多只能提供炸秋葵。现在有了有机咖啡和工匠山羊奶酪。我们头顶挂着一个标志，上面写着“低语鸽牧场”。

00:54:12.980 --> 00:54:24.670
<v David Sedaris>And just as I thought that we might be anywhere, I noticed that the music piped through the speakers was Christian. The new kind, which says that Jesus is awesome.
<v David Sedaris>就在我认为我们可能身处任何地方时，我注意到通过扬声器播放的音乐是基督教音乐。新式的那种，说耶稣很棒。

00:54:24.670 --> 00:54:35.190
<v David Sedaris>Hugh brought my father a plastic cup of water. "You OK, Lou?" "Fine, " my father answered.
<v David Sedaris>休给我父亲拿了一杯塑料杯水。“你还好吗，卢？”“还好，”我父亲回答。

00:54:35.190 --> 00:54:59.820
<v David Sedaris>"Why do you think she did it?" I asked as we stepped back into the sunlight, for that's all any of us were thinking, had been thinking since we got the news. Mustn't Tiffany have hoped that whatever pills she'd taken wouldn't be strong enough? And that her failed attempt would lead her back into our fold? How could anyone purposefully leave us, us of all people?
<v David Sedaris>“你觉得她为什么这么做？”我们回到阳光下时我问道，因为这就是我们所有人都在想的，自从得到消息后就一直在想。蒂芙尼难道不希望她服用的任何药片都不会那么强烈吗？她失败的尝试会引导她回到我们的怀抱？怎么可能有人会有意离开我们，我们这些人中的所有人？

00:54:59.820 --> 00:55:20.350
<v David Sedaris>This is how I thought of it. For though I've often lost faith in myself, I've never lost faith in my family. In my certainty that we are fundamentally better than everyone else. It's an archaic belief, one I haven't seriously reconsidered since my late teens, but still I hold it.
<v David Sedaris>这就是我思考它的方式。因为虽然我经常失去对自己的信心，但我从没失去对家人的信心。我确信我们从根本上比其他人更好。这是一个古老的信念，我从十几岁后期以来就没认真重新考虑过，但我仍然持有它。

00:55:20.350 --> 00:55:34.450
<v David Sedaris>Ours is the only club I'd ever wanted to be a member of, so I couldn't imagine quitting. Backing off for a year or two was understandable. But to want out so badly that you'd take your own life?
<v David Sedaris>我们是我想加入的唯一俱乐部，所以我无法想象退出。退出一两年是可以理解的。但想离开到那种程度，以至于你会结束自己的生命？

00:55:34.450 --> 00:55:51.080
<v David Sedaris>"I don't know that it had anything to do with us," my father said. But how could it have not? Doesn't the blood of every suicide splash back on our faces?
<v David Sedaris>“我不知道这与我们有什么关系，”我父亲说。但怎么可能没有呢？难道每个自杀者的鲜血不是都溅回了我们的脸上吗？

00:55:51.080 --> 00:56:08.060
<v David Sedaris>At the far end of the parking lot was a stand selling reptiles. In giant tanks were two pythons, each as big around as a fire hose. The heat seemed to suit them, and I watched as they raised their heads, testing the screen ceilings.
<v David Sedaris>停车场尽头是一个卖爬行动物的摊位。巨大的水箱里有两条蟒蛇，每条都有消防水管那么粗。热量似乎很适合它们，我看着它们抬起头，试探着屏幕天花板。

00:56:08.060 --> 00:56:27.790
<v David Sedaris>Beside the snakes was a low pen corralling an alligator with its mouth banded shut. It wasn't full grown, but perhaps an adolescent, around three feet long and grumpy looking. A girl had stuck her arm through the wire and was stroking the thing's back while it glared, seething.
<v David Sedaris>蛇旁边是一个低矮的围栏，关着一只嘴巴被束缚住的短吻鳄。它还没完全长大，但可能是一只未成年，大约三英尺长，看起来脾气暴躁。一个女孩把手臂伸过金属丝，正在抚摸那东西的背，而它怒视着，怒气冲冲。

00:56:27.790 --> 00:56:41.240
<v David Sedaris>"I'd like to buy everything here, just so I could kill it," I said. My father mopped his forehead with Kleenex. "I'm with you, brother."
<v David Sedaris>“我想买下这里的一切，只是为了能杀掉它，”我说。我父亲用纸巾擦了擦前额。“我站在你这边，兄弟。”

00:56:41.240 --> 00:57:00.730
<v David Sedaris>When we were young and would set off for the beach, I'd look out the window at all the landmarks we drove by-- the Purina silo on the south side of Raleigh, the Klan billboard-- knowing that when we passed them a week later, I'd be miserable. Our vacation over, now there'd be nothing to live for until Christmas.
<v David Sedaris>当我们年轻并出发去海滩时，我会从窗户看着我们经过的所有地标——罗利南侧的普瑞纳筒仓，三K党的广告牌——知道一周后我们经过它们时，我会感到痛苦。我们的假期结束了，现在直到圣诞节没什么可活的了。

00:57:00.730 --> 00:57:14.350
<v David Sedaris>My life is much fuller than it was back then, yet this return felt no different. "What time is it?" I asked Amy. And instead of saying "Who cares?" she said, "You tell me. You're the one with the watch on."
<v David Sedaris>我的生活比那时充实得多，但这种回归感觉没什么不同。“现在几点了？”我问艾米。她没有说“谁在乎？”，而是说，“你自己看吧。你才是戴着手表的那个人。”

00:57:14.350 --> 00:57:29.060
<v David Sedaris>At the airport a few hours later, I picked sand from my pockets and thought of our final moments at the beach house I'd bought. I was on the front porch with Phyllis, who had just locked the door, and we turned to see the others in the driveway below us.
<v David Sedaris>几小时后在机场，我从口袋里捡起沙子，想起我们在我买下的海滩别墅里的最后时刻。我和菲莉丝在门廊上，她刚锁上门，我们转过身看到下面车道上的其他人。

00:57:29.060 --> 00:57:38.920
<v David Sedaris>"So is that one of your sisters?" she asked, pointing at Gretchen. "It is," I said. "And so are the two women standing on either side of her."
<v David Sedaris>“那是你的姐妹之一吗？”她指着格雷琴问。“是的，”我说。“站在她两边的两个女人也是。”

00:57:38.920 --> 00:57:47.260
<v David Sedaris>"Then you've got your brother," she observed. "That makes five. Wow, now that's a big family."
<v David Sedaris>“然后你有你的兄弟，”她观察到。“那加起来五个。哇，那是一个大家庭。”

00:57:47.260 --> 00:58:09.030
<v David Sedaris>I looked at the sun-baked cars we would soon be climbing into, furnaces, every one of them, and said, "Yes. It certainly is."
<v David Sedaris>我看着我们很快就要爬进去的被阳光烘烤的汽车，每一辆都是熔炉，说，“是的。确实是。”

00:58:09.030 --> 00:58:16.900
<v Ira Glass>David Sedaris is the author of many books, most recently, Let's Explore Diabetes With Owls .
<v Ira Glass>大卫·塞达里斯是许多书的作者，最近的一本是《让我们用猫头鹰探索糖尿病》。

00:58:16.900 --> 00:59:15.840
<v Ira Glass>[MUSIC - "USED TO BE" BY BEACH HOUSE]
<v Ira Glass>[音乐 - BEACH HOUSE的《过去》]

00:59:15.840 --> 01:00:12.730
